window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["ai-gen-history-revolt-of-1857-causes-key-figures-consequences"] = `
<div class="revision-card">
<h1>Revolt of 1857 – The First War of Indian Independence</h1>

<h2>Overview</h2>
<p>The <strong>Revolt of 1857</strong>, popularly called the <strong>First War of Independence</strong> by <span style='color: var(--warning);'>Vinayak Damodar Savarkar</span>, was a major uprising against the <strong>British East India Company</strong>. Though it failed militarily, it marked the end of Company rule and the beginning of direct British Crown control.</p>

<h2>Causes of the Revolt</h2>

<h3>Political Causes</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Doctrine of Lapse</strong> – Used by <span style='color: var(--warning);'>Lord Dalhousie</span> to annex states without natural heirs (applied to <strong>Anglo-Garhwal</strong>, <strong>Jaipur</strong>, <strong>Sati</strong> ignored in some cases).</li>
<li>Annexations under <strong>Subsidiary Alliance System</strong> increased resentment among rulers.</li>
<li>Removal of <strong>Nawab of Bengal</strong>’s authority after <span style='color: var(--warning);'>Siraj-ud-Daulah</span>’s death in <span style='color: var(--warning);'>1757</span>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Economic Causes</h_value>
<ul>
<li>Heavy <strong>land revenue settlements</strong> ruined peasants and zamindars.</li>
<li><strong>Commercialization of agriculture</strong> led to grain shortages and famines.</li>
<li>Drain of wealth to Britain through <strong>home charges</strong> and <strong>tribute payments</strong>.</li>
<li>Deindustrialization of Indian handicrafts due to machine-made British goods.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Social & Religious Causes</h3>
<ul>
<li>British interference in <strong>religious customs</strong> – <strong>Sati</strong>, <strong>Thuggee</strong>, and <strong>infant marriage</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>Macaulay's Education Policy</strong> created a class alienated from traditional values.</li>
<li>Misinterpretation of <strong>Rajput and Muslim</strong> caste sensibilities by British.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Military Causes</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Enfield Rifle</strong> cartridges greased with <strong>cow and buffalo fat</strong>, offending both <strong>Hindus and Muslims</strong>.</li>
<li>Discontent among <strong>sepoys</strong> due to slow promotions and discriminatory policies.</li>
<li>Capture of <strong>Delhi Durbar</strong> troops after <strong>Ranjit Singh’s</strong> death weakened Sikh forces.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Immediate Trigger</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong> Mangal Pandey’s revolt</strong> in <span style='color: var(--warning);'>March 1857</span> at <strong>Meerut</strong> – he attacked his officers, shouting “<em>Kaerat Mussalmān Hai?</em>”</li>
<li>He was sentenced to death; hanged on <span style='color: var(--warning);'>April 19, 1857</span>, becoming a martyr.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Key Figures of the Revolt</h2>

<table border="1" cellpadding="8">
<tr><th>Leader</th><th>Region</th><th>Role / Contribution</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Bahadur Shah Zafar</strong></td><td>Delhi</td><td>Declared Emperor; symbolic figurehead for revolt</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Rani Lakshmibai</strong></td><td>Jhansi</td><td>Leadership in battle; died fighting British</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Nana Sahib</strong></td><td>Cawnpore</td><td>Organized revolt; responsible for <strong>Cawnpore Massacre</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Tipu Sultan’s son</strong></td><td>South India</td><td>Fought in <strong>Bangalore</strong> uprising</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Kuaiyyal Singh</strong></td><td>Army</td><td>Key conspirator among officers</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Ramkrishna Yadav</strong></td><td>Bihar</td><td>Exposed conspiracy prelude</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Shivdev Singh</strong></td><td>United Provinces</td><td>Co-conspirator in early planning</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Important British Figures</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Lord Canning</strong> – Viceroy; offered <strong>Delhi House Settlement</strong> to rulers who remained loyal.</li>
<li><strong>Lord Lawrence</strong> – Commander-in-chief during later phase.</li>
<li><strong>General Havelock</strong> and <strong>_general_outram_</strong> – Led relief forces to <strong>Cawnpore</strong> and <strong>Lucknow</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>John Nicholson</strong> – Suppressed revolt in Punjab and Delhi.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Chronology of Events</h2>
<ol>
<li><span style='color: var(--warning);'>March 29, 1857</span> – Mangal Pandey’s attack at Meerut.</li>
<li><span style='color: var(--warning);'>May 10, 1857</span> – Meerut sepoys march to Delhi and declare Bahadur Shah II as Emperor.</li>
<li><span style='color: var(--warning);'>June 1857</span> – <strong>Battle of Delhi</strong>; <strong>Rani Lakshmibai</strong> escapes Jhansi.</li>
<li><span style='color: var(--warning);'>July 1857</span> – Siege of <strong>Lucknow</strong> begins.</li>
<li><span style='color: var(--warning);'>November 1857</span> – Relief of Lucknow by <strong>Havelock</strong> and <strong>Outram</strong>.</li>
<li><span style='color: var(--warning);'>September 1857</span> – Fall of <strong>Cawnpore</strong>; massacre at <strong>Sati lake</strong>.</li>
<li><span style='color: var(--warning);'>September 20, 1857</span> – <strong>Nana Sahib</strong> flees to <strong>Nepal</strong>.</li>
<li><span style='color: var(--warning);'>September 1858</span> – <strong>Bahadur Shah Zafar</strong> captured in <strong>Patna</strong>, exiled to <strong>Rangoon</strong>.</li>
<li><span style='color: var(--warning);'>August 1858</span> – <strong>Governor-General</strong> title abolished; Queen’s Proclamation issued.</li>
<li><span style='color: var(--warning);'>20 July 1858</span> – Formal transfer of power to Crown via <strong>Act of 1858</strong>.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Consequences of the Revolt</h2>

<h3>Political Consequences</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>End of East India Company</strong> – dissolved by <strong>Act of 1858</strong>.</li>
<li>Establishment of <strong>British Raj</strong> – direct Crown rule from <span style='color: var(--warning);'>1858</span>.</li>
<li><strong>Queen Victoria’s Proclamation</strong> (<span style='color: var(--warning);'>1858</span>) promised non-interference in princely states and equal treatment of all religions.</li>
<li>Policy of <strong>associating Indians with administration</strong> initiated.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Military Reforms</h3>
<ul>
<li>Ratio of European to Indian troops increased.</li>
<li>Recruitment shifted from <strong>Brahmins/Kshatriyas</strong> to <strong>martial races</strong> like <strong>Gurkhas</strong>, <strong>Sikh</strong>, <strong>Rajputana</strong>.</li>
<li>Artillery transferred entirely to European hands.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Administrative Changes</strong></h3>
<ul>
<li>Council system strengthened under <strong>Indian Councils Act</strong>.</li>
<li>Promulgation of <strong>vernacular press policy</strong> to suppress nationalist publications.</li>
<li>Emphasis on <strong>associating princes</strong> in governance through <strong>princely states</strong> framework.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Social & Cultural Impact</h3>
<ul>
<li>Growht of <strong>national consciousness</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>Partition of Bengal</strong> delayed; later reversed under pressure.</li>
<li><strong>Reorganization of army</strong> created long-term loyalty issues.</li>
<li><strong>Revival of communal politics</strong> due to divide-and-rule tactics.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Failures of the Revolt</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Lack of unity</strong> among rebels – no central command structure.</li>
<li><strong>Communal divisions</strong> – Hindus and Muslims acted separately.</li>
<li><strong>No coordination</strong> between civilian leaders and military commanders.</li>
<li><strong>Limited geographical spread</strong> – mostly confined to North and Central India.</li>
<li><strong>Poor communication</strong> networks hindered joint operations.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Significance & Legacy</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>End of feudalism</strong> and rise of modern nationalism.</li>
<li>Marked turning point towards <strong>organized freedom struggle</strong>.</li>
<li>Exposed futility of <strong>co-operative politics</strong> with British.</li>
<li>Set stage for formation of <strong>Indian National Congress</strong> (<span style='color: var(--warning);'>1885</span>).</li>
<li>Influenced later revolutionaries like <strong>Bhagat Singh</strong>, <strong>Khudiram Bose</strong>, etc.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Exam Tips & Mnemonics</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mnemonic for causes</strong>: <em>"PEMCE"</em> – Political, Economic, Military, Cultural, External trigger.</li>
<li><strong>Bahadur Shah Zafar</strong> was the last Mughal emperor; remember he was defeated at <strong>Independence Struggle Trials</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>Rani Lakshmibai’s</strong> famous quote: “<em>Khoob lade mardani, woh toh Jhansi wali Rani thi</em>.”</li>
<li><strong>Act of 1858</strong> passed on <span style='color: var(--warning);'>August 2, 1858</span>; key date for NDA/CDS exams.</li>
<li><strong>Nana Sahib’s real name</strong> – <strong>Dada Buxar</strong>; relation to <strong>Baji Rao II</strong>.</li>
</ul>

</div>
`;

