window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["ai-gen-geography-rocks-minerals-igneous-sedimentary-metamorphic-rock-cycle"] = `
<div class="revision-card">

<h2>Rocks & Minerals – Physical Geography</h2>

<h3>What Are Rocks?</h3>
<p>A <strong>rock</strong> is a natural solid material formed from minerals or mineraloids. Rocks are the external manifestation of Earth’s internal processes. Understanding rocks is crucial for NDA/CDS/AFCAT exams as they form the basis of physical geography, engineering services, and defence planning (e.g., construction materials, resource identification).</p>

<h3>The Rock Cycle</h3>
<p>The <strong>rock cycle</strong> illustrates how rocks transform from one type to another through geological processes over millions of years. Energy from the Sun and Earth’s internal heat drive this cycle.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Key Processes:</strong> Weathering, erosion, deposition, compaction, cementation, melting, crystallization</li>
</ul>
<img src="rockcycle.png" alt="Rock Cycle Diagram" style="width:100%;max-width:400px;">

<h3>Three Major Types of Rocks</h3>

<h4>1. Igneous Rocks</h4>
<p>Formed by cooling and solidification of magma or lava.</p>
<table border="1" cellpadding="5">
<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Intrusive (Plutonic)</th><th>Extrusive (Volcanic)</th></tr>
<tr><td>Cooling Location</td><td>Below surface</td><td>On surface</td></tr>
<tr><td>Crystal Size</td><td>Coarse-grained</td><td>Fine-grained or glassy</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lava Flow</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Examples</td><td>Granite, Gabbro, Diorite</td><td>Basalt, Rhyolite, Andesite</td></tr>
</table>
<p><strong>Mnemonic:</strong> “I Go Down Into Volcanoes, Explosively Erupting” → Igneous, Granite, Intrusive, Volcanic, Extrusive, Eruption</p>
<p><span style='color: var(--warning);'>Fun Fact:</span> Over 75% of Earth’s surface is covered by igneous rocks.</p>

<h4>2. Sedimentary Rocks</h4>
<p>Formed by accumulation and lithification of sediments.</p>
<table border="1" cellpadding="5">
<tr><th>Origin Type</th><th>Description</th><th>Examples</th></tr>
<tr><td>Detrital</td><td>Weathered fragments</td><td>Sandstone, Shale, Conglomerate</td></tr>
<tr><td>Chemical</td><td>Precipitation from water</td><td>Limestone, Rock Salt, Gypsum</td></tr>
<tr><td>Organic</td><td>Biological origin</td><td>Coal, Chalk, Shell Limestone</td></tr>
</table>
<p><strong>Key Terms:</strong> <strong> lithification</strong> = compaction + cementation</p>
<p><span style='color: var(--warning);'>Timeline:</span> Most sedimentary rocks form within <span style='color: var(--warning);'>10⁶–10⁸</span> years.</p>

<h4>3. Metamorphic Rocks</h4>
<p>Formed by alteration of existing rocks under heat, pressure, and chemically active fluids.</p>
<table border="1" cellpadding="5">
<tr><th>Type</th><th>Texture</th><th>Forming Conditions</th><th>Examples</th></tr>
<tr><td>Foliated</td><td>Layered/banded</td><td> Directed pressure</td><td>Gneiss, Schist, Slate</td></tr>
<tr><td>Non-foliated</td><td> Massive</td><td>Uniform pressure</td><td>Marble, Quartzite</td></tr>
</table>
<p><strong>Mnemonic:</strong> “Silly Geologists Often Make Strange Sounds” → Slate → Gneiss → Marble → Schist → Quartzite</p>

<h3>Minerals vs. Rocks</h3>
<table border="1" cellpadding="5">
<tr><th>Rock</th><th>Mineral</th></tr>
<tr><td>Combination of minerals</td><td>Pure substance with definite chemical composition</td></tr>
<tr><td>No fixed composition</td><td>Fixed composition</td></tr>
<tr><td>Can be any size/shape</td><td>Has specific physical properties</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Physical Properties of Minerals</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Color</strong> – Not always reliable (e.g., quartz appears in many colors)</li>
<li><strong>Streak</strong> – Color of powdered mineral</li>
<li><strong>Luster</strong> – How light reflects off surface (metallic vs. non-metallic)</li>
<li><strong>Hardness</strong> – Measured on <strong>Mohs Scale</strong></li>
<li><strong>Streak Plate Test</strong> – Unglazed porcelain plate used to determine streak</li>
<li><strong>Fracture/Cleavage</strong> – Breakage pattern</li>
</ul>

<h3>Mohs Hardness Scale (1–10)</h3>
<table border="1" cellpadding="5">
<tr><th>Number</th><th>Mineral</th><th>Common Item for Comparison</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>Talc</td><td>Fingernail (2.5)</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>Gypsum</td><td>Soft lead pencil</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>Calcite</td><td>Copper coin</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>Fluorite</td><td>Iron nail</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>Apatite</td><td>Glass</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>Feldspar</td><td>Steel knife</td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>Quartz</td><td>Steel file</td></tr>
<tr><td>8</td><td>Topaz</td><td>Hard steel</td></tr>
<tr><td>9</td><td>Corundum</td><td>Aluminum oxide</td></tr>
<tr><td>10</td><td>Diamond</td><td>Nothing scratches it</td></tr>
</table>
<p><strong>Mnemonic:</strong> “The Great Big Dogs Eat Chicken Bones Daily” → Talc, Gypsum, Calcite, Fluorite, Apatite, Feldspar, Quartz, Topaz, Corundum, Diamond</p>

<h3>Economic Importance of Rocks & Minerals</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Bauxite</strong> (sedimentary) – Source of aluminum</li>
<li><strong>Iron ore</strong> (metamorphosed banded iron formation) – Steel production</li>
<li><strong>Coal</strong> (organic sedimentary) – Power generation</li>
<li><strong>Granite</strong> (igneous) – Construction material</li>
<li><strong>Limestone</strong> (sedimentary) – Cement, lime, steel industry flux</li>
<li><strong>Mica</strong> (metamorphic/sedimentary) – Electrical insulation</li>
</ul>

<h3>Exam-Focused Key Points</h3>
<ul>
<li><span style='color: var(--warning);'>0°C to 1000°C+</span> – Temperature range during metamorphism</li>
<li><span style='color: var(--warning);'>10–30 km depth</span> – Typical depth for regional metamorphism</li>
<li><strong>Protolith</strong> – Original rock before metamorphism</li>
<li><strong>Hornedfel</strong> – Example of hornfels; non-foliated, contact metamorphic rock</li>
<li><strong>Phonolite</strong> – Rare dark-colored volcanic rock</li>
<li><strong>Obsidian</strong> – Volcanic glass, very sharp edges</li>
<li><strong>Pumice</strong> – Light, floating volcanic rock with gas bubbles</li>
<li><strong>Marble</strong> – Met from limestone; used in sculptures (e.g., Taj Mahal)</li>
</ul>

<h3>Defence Applications</h3>
<ul>
<li>Granite quarries used for military infrastructure</li>
<li>Basaltic rocks ideal for airstrips due to hardness</li>
<li>Limestone can dissolve → risk of karst topography affecting underground installations</li>
<li>Sandstone is porous → not suitable for foundations in wet conditions</li>
<li>Clay-rich soils expand when wet → unsuitable for roads/bases</li>
</ul>

<h3>Quick Recap Quiz (Answers Below)</h3>
<ol>
<li>Which scale measures mineral hardness?</li>
<li>Name two foliated metamorphic rocks.</li>
<li>What is the main process converting shale into slate?</li>
<li>Granite is an example of which rock type?</li>
<li>What is the term for combining sediments into rock?</li>
</ol>

<h4>Answers:</h4>
<ol>
<li>Mohs Hardness Scale</li>
<li>Gneiss, Schist, Slate</li>
<li>Regional metamorphism with directed pressure</li>
<li>Igneous (Intrusive/Plutonic)</li>
<li>Lithification</li>
</ol>

<p><strong>Tip:</strong> In exams, always link rock types to their forming environment and textures—this helps solve matching-type questions quickly.</p>

</div>
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["ai-gen-geography-earthquakes-volcanoes-causes-types-measurement-distribution"] = `
<div class="revision-card">
<h2>Earthquakes & Volcanoes – Causes, Types, Measurement, Distribution</h2>

<h3>1. <strong>Earthquakes</strong></h3>
<p><strong>Definition:</strong> Shaking of earth due to release of energy in Earth’s <strong>lithosphere</strong>.</p>

<h4>Causes:</h4>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Tectonic plate movements:</strong> Built-up stress along plate boundaries (convergent, divergent, transform)</li>
  <li><strong>Volcanic activity:</strong> Magma movement beneath volcanoes</li>
  <li><strong>Human activities:</strong> Mining, reservoirs, nuclear tests</li>
  <li><strong>Landslides:</strong> Sudden displacement of rock/debris</li>
</ul>

<h4>Types:</h4>
<table border="1">
  <tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Interplate</strong></td><td>Between two tectonic plates</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Intraplate</strong></td><td>Within a single plate (less common)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Volcanic</strong></td><td>Associated with magma movement</td></tr>
</table>

<h4>Measurement:</h4>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Richter Scale (1932):</strong> Logarithmic (magnitude 0–10). Each step = <span style='color: var(--warning);'>10× amplitude</span></li>
  <li><strong>Moment Magnitude Scale (Mw):</strong> Modern standard; measures <strong>slip × rupture length</strong>. Used for large quakes (e.g., 2004 Indian Ocean quake: <span style='color: var(--warning);'>Mw 9.1–9.3</span>).</li>
</ul>

<h4>Key Terms:</h4>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Focus:</strong> Point where rupture starts underground</li>
  <li><strong>Fault line:</strong> Fracture in Earth’s crust</li>
  <li><strong>Epicenter:</strong> Surface point directly above focus</li>
</ul>

<h4>Distribution:</h4>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Pacific Ring of Fire:</strong> <span style='color: var(--warning);'>90%</span> of global earthquakes, <span style='color: var(--warning);'>75%</span> of active volcanoes</li>
  <li><strong>Convergent boundaries:</strong> India-Asia collision (Himalayas), Peru-Chile trench</li>
  <li><strong>Mid-Ocean Ridges:</strong> Divergent spreading centers (e.g., Atlantic)</li>
</ul>

<h4>Notable Examples:</h4>
<ul>
  <li><strong>2011 Tōhoku, Japan:</strong> <span style='color: var(--warning);'>Mw 9.0</span>, triggered tsunami</li>
  <li><strong>2010 Haiti Earthquake:</strong> <span style='color: var(--warning);'>Mw 7.0</span>, devastating in urban area</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. <strong>Volcanoes</strong></h3>
<p><strong>Definition:</strong> Natural openings in crust where <strong>magma</strong> (molten rock) erupts to surface.</p>

<h4>Causes:</h4>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Magma formation:</strong> Cooling/partial melting of mantle material due to <strong>decompression melting</strong>, <strong>flux melting</strong>, or <strong>heat exchange</strong></li>
  <li><strong>Tectonic settings:</strong> Divergent (mid-ocean ridges), convergent (subduction zones), hotspot (isolated volcanoes)</li>
</ul>

<h4>Types:</h4>
<table border="1">
  <tr><th>Type</th><th>Formation</th><th>Eruption Style</th></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Shield Volcano</strong></td><td>Basaltic magma (low viscosity)</td><td>Gentle, fluid lava flows (e.g., Mauna Loa)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Stratovolcano (Composite)</strong></td><td>Andesitic/dacitic magma (high viscosity)</td><td>Explosive, layered (e.g., Mount Fuji, Vesuvius)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Cinder Cone</strong></td><td>Basaltic to andesitic</td><td>Short-lived, steep (e.g., Paricutin)</td></tr>
</table>

<h4>Mnemonic:</strong> “<strong>S</strong>mooth <strong>H</strong>ow <strong>S</strong>tacks” for Shield (Smooth), H (Stratovolcano/Steep), Cones (Small)</h4>

<h4>Measurement:</h4>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Volcanic Explosivity Index (VEI):</strong> Ranges <span style='color: var(--warning);'>0–8</span>, based on eruption volume, column height, and destructive duration</li>
  <li><strong>Hazard scale (HAZMAP):</strong> For lahar, pyroclastic flow risks</li>
</ul>

<h4>Distribution:</h4>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Ring of Fire:</strong> Pacific Ocean edges (subduction zones like Kamchatka, Aleutians)</li>
  <li><strong>Mid-Atlantic Ridge:</strong> Divergent (e.g., Iceland)</li>
  <li><strong>Hotspot):</strong> Hawaii (Kea), Galápagos, Yellowstone</li>
</ul>

<h4>Notable Examples:</h4>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Krakatoa (1883):</strong> <span style='color: var(--warning);'>VEI 6</span>, caused <span style='color: var(--warning);'>global cooling</span></li>
  <li><strong>Mount St. Helens (1980):</strong> <span style='color: var(--warning);'>VEI 5</span>, lateral blast killed 57 people</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. <strong>Key Comparisons</strong></h3>
<table border="1">
  <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Earthquake</th><th>Volcano</th></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Energy Source</strong></td><td>Elastic rebound (stress release)</td><td>Magma ascent & pressure buildup</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Early Warning</strong></td><td>Sensitive seismic networks (e.g., Japan’s Hi-net)</td><td>Seismic precursors, gas emissions</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Scale Used</strong></td><td>Richter/MW scales</td><td>VEI, HAZMAP</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>4. <strong>Exam Tips</strong></h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Memorize dates/numbers:</strong> 1883 Krakatoa (<span style='color: var(--warning);'>VEI 6</span>), 2004 tsunami (<span style='color: var(--warning);'>Mw 9.1–9.3</span>), 2011 Japan (<span style='color: var(--warning);'>Mw 9.0</span>)</li>
  <li><strong>Identify types:</strong> Shield (smooth, basaltic), Stratovolcano (steep, explosive), Cone (small)</li>
  <li><strong>Focus on Ring of Fire:</strong> 90% of quakes occur here; key examples include Chile, Indonesia, Japan</li>
  <li><strong>Contrasts:</strong> Differentiate Richter (local) vs. Moment scale (global accuracy)</li>
</ul>

<h3>5. <strong>Important Scientists & Concepts</strong></h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>John Richer (1885):</strong> Developed Richter scale</li>
  <li><strong>Plate Tectonics Theory (1960s):</strong> Explains earthquake/volcano distributions</li>
  <li><strong>Foehn Effect:</strong> Volcanic aerosols affect global climate (e.g., Krakatoa)</li>
</ul>

<strong>Remember:</strong> Earthquakes can occur anywhere on plate boundaries; volcanoes require magma pathways. Always associate types with their geological settings and hazards!</div>
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["ai-gen-geography-plate-tectonics-plates-boundaries-sea-floor-spreading"] = `
<div class="revision-card">
  <h1>Physical Geography – Plate Tectonics</h1>
  
  <h2>1. What is Plate Tectonics?</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Plate Tectonics</strong> is the scientific theory that explains the movement of the Earth’s lithosphere, which is broken into a number of rigid slabs called <strong>tectonic plates</strong>. These plates float on the semi‑fluid asthenosphere and interact at their edges, giving rise to most of the planet’s major geological features and seismic activity.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Types of Tectonic Plates</h2>
  <p>There are three broad categories of plates based on their composition and location:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Continental plates</strong> – composed mainly of granitic crust (average thickness 30‑50 km).</li>
    <li><strong>Oceanic plates</strong> – basaltic crust, thinner (5‑10 km) and denser.</li>
    <li><strong>Hybrid plates</strong> – contain both continental and oceanic fragments (e.g., the Indian Plate).</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Major Plates (as per latest IUGG model)</h3>
  <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%;">
    <tr style="background:#f2f2f2;">
      <th>Plate</th>
      <th>Dominant Region</th>
      <th>Key Features</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Pacific Plate</strong></td>
      <td>Pacific Ocean basin</td>
      <td>Largest plate; hosts the “Ring of Fire” – intense volcanic & seismic activity.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>North American Plate</strong></td>
      <td>North America, part of Atlantic Ocean</td>
      <td>Contains both continental and oceanic crust; includes the Mid‑Atlantic Ridge.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Eurasian Plate</strong></td>
      <td>Europe & Asia (excluding Indian subcontinent)</td>
      <td>Hosts the Himalayas (collision zone with Indian Plate).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Indian Plate</strong></td>
      <td>Indian subcontinent & Indian Ocean</td>
      <td>Moving northward at ~5 cm/yr; colliding with Eurasian Plate.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>African Plate</strong></td>
      <td>Africa & surrounding oceans</td>
      <td>Slowly rotating clockwise; includes the East African Rift.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>South American Plate</strong></td>
      <td>South America & part of Atlantic</td>
      <td>Hosts the Andes – a classic example of continental‑oceanic convergence.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Antarctic Plate</strong></td>
      <td>Antarctica & surrounding Southern Ocean</td>
      <td>Relatively stable; contains the West Antarctic Rift System.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Australian Plate</strong></td>
      <td>Australia, New Zealand, part of Indian Ocean</td>
      <td>Moving north‑eastward; interacts with Pacific Plate at the Tonga‑Kermadec trench.</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h2>3. Plate Boundaries – Classification & Characteristics</h2>
  <p>Boundaries are zones where plates interact. Three primary types exist, each with distinctive landforms, seismic patterns, and volcanic activity.</p>

  <h3>3.1 Divergent Boundaries (Constructive)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Plates move **away** from each other.</li>
    <li>New crust is created by upwelling magma – <strong>sea‑floor spreading</strong>.</li>
    <li>Typical features: mid‑ocean ridges, rift valleys, volcanic fissures.</li>
    <li>Earthquakes are generally shallow and of moderate magnitude.</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>Key Example: Mid‑Atlantic Ridge</h4>
  <p>
    Extends from the Arctic Ocean to the Southern Atlantic. The Atlantic Ocean widens at an average rate of <span style='color: var(--warning);'>2.5 cm/yr</span>. This ridge is a classic textbook illustration of divergent activity.
  </p>

  <h3>3.2 Convergent Boundaries (Destructive)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Plates move **toward** each other.</li>
    <li>One plate is usually forced beneath another – <strong>subduction</strong>.</li>
    <li>Three sub‑types:
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Oceanic‑Oceanic</strong> – forms volcanic island arcs (e.g., Japanese Archipelago).</li>
        <li><strong>Oceanic‑Continental</strong> – creates coastal mountain ranges and volcanic arcs (e.g., Andes, Cascades).</li>
        <li><strong>Continental‑Continental</strong> – produces massive fold‑mountain belts (e.g., Himalayas).</li>
      </ol>
    </li>
    <li>Associated phenomena: deep‑focus earthquakes, volcanic activity, trench formation.</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>Mnemonic for Sub‑types: “**O**ceanic‑**O**ceanic = **I**slands, **O**ceanic‑**C**ontinental = **C**oast‑mountains, **C**ontinental‑**C**ontinental = **H**igh‑mountains”</h4>

  <h3>3.3 Transform Boundaries (Conservative)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Plates slide **past** each other horizontally.</li>
    <li>No crust is created or destroyed.</li>
    <li>Typical features: linear fault zones, offset streams, pull‑apart basins.</li>
    <li>Earthquakes are shallow but can be very powerful (e.g., 1906 San Francisco quake).</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>Key Example: San Andreas Fault (California)</h4>
  <p>
    Marks the boundary between the Pacific and North American plates. Slip rate ≈ <span style='color: var(--warning);'>3 cm/yr</span>.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Sea‑Floor Spreading – Mechanism & Evidence</h2>
  <p>
    First proposed by <strong>Harry Hess (1962)</strong> and later substantiated by magnetic anomaly studies. The process explains how new oceanic crust forms and pushes older crust away from the ridge axis.
  </p>

  <h3>4.1 Mechanism</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Heat from the mantle creates upwelling magma at a divergent ridge.</li>
    <li>Molten basalt erupts, solidifies, and adds a new layer to the ocean floor.</li>
    <li>As more magma rises, the older crust is pushed laterally, creating symmetrical age patterns on either side of the ridge.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>4.2 Evidence Supporting Sea‑Floor Spreading</h3>
  <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; margin-top:10px;">
    <tr style="background:#e8f4ff;">
      <th>Evidence</th>
      <th>Explanation</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Magnetic Striping</strong></td>
      <td>Alternating normal and reversed magnetic anomalies parallel to ridges record Earth’s geomagnetic reversals; symmetric about the ridge axis.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Age of Oceanic Crust</strong></td>
      <td>Radiometric dating shows crust is youngest at the ridge and oldest farthest away (max ≈ <span style='color: var(--warning);'>200 Ma</span>).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Heat Flow</strong></td>
      <td>Higher heat flow near ridges, decreasing outward, consistent with upwelling magma.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Bathymetry</strong></td>
      <td>Mid‑ocean ridges are elevated (2‑3 km) due to thermal buoyancy of hot crust.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Seafloor Sedimentation</strong></td>
      <td>Thinner sediment cover near ridges, thicker toward continental margins, matching age progression.</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h3>4.3 Rate of Spreading</h3>
  <p>Spreading rates are classified as:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Fast</strong> – > <span style='color: var(--warning);'>9 cm/yr</span> (e.g., East Pacific Rise).</li>
    <li><strong>Intermediate</strong> – 5‑9 cm/yr (e.g., Mid‑Atlantic Ridge).</li>
    <li><strong>Slow</strong> – < <span style='color: var(--warning);'>5 cm/yr</span> (e.g., Southwest Indian Ridge).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>5. Plate Motions – Global Patterns</h2>
  <p>Plate motions are driven by mantle convection, slab pull, ridge push, and trench suction. The net motion can be visualized using a <strong>global plate motion map</strong> (e.g., NUVEL‑1A model).</p>

  <h3>5.1 Major Motion Vectors (approx.)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Pacific Plate – moves <strong>north‑west</strong> at ~<span style='color: var(--warning);'>7 cm/yr</span>.</li>
    <li>Indian Plate – moves <strong>north‑north‑east</strong> at ~<span style='color: var(--warning);'>5 cm/yr</span>.</li>
    <li>African Plate – drifts <strong>north‑east</strong> at ~<span style='color: var(--warning);'>2 cm/yr</span>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>6. Important Terms & Mnemonics</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Ridge Push</strong> – gravity‑driven force from elevated mid‑ocean ridges.</li>
    <li><strong>Slab Pull</strong> – sinking of dense, cold oceanic lithosphere into the mantle; the most powerful driver.</li>
    <li><strong>Transform Fault</strong> – a strike‑slip fault that offsets a ridge or trench.</li>
    <li><strong>Subduction Zone</strong> – a trench‑bounded area where one plate descends beneath another.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Mnemonic for Forces</h3>
  <p><em>“**R**idges **P**ush, **S**labs **P**ull – **T**ransforms **S**lide”</em></p>

  <h2>7. Exam Tips for NDA/CDS/AFCAT</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Never memorize only names.</strong> Relate each boundary type to its characteristic landforms and earthquake depth.</li>
    <li>For **dates/numbers**, always write them in the <span style='color: var(--warning);'>highlighted format</span> during practice – it trains you to recall exact figures (e.g., spreading rates, ages).</li>
    <li>Use **tables** in your answer sheet: a 2‑column table (Boundary – Feature) fetches quick marks.</li>
    <li>Remember the **“Three Types – Three Features”** rule:
      <ul>
        <li>Divergent – <strong>New crust</strong> (mid‑ocean ridges).</li>
        <li>Convergent – <strong>Destruction</strong> (trenches, arcs, mountains).</li>
        <li>Transform – <strong>Sliding</strong> (faults, earthquakes).</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>Practice drawing a **simple world map** showing major plates and boundary types – a 5‑minute sketch can earn up to 2 marks in map‑based questions.</li>
    <li>For **sea‑floor spreading**, always mention the four lines of evidence; exam setters love “magnetic striping + age progression”.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>8. Quick Revision Summary (Bullet Form)</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Plate Types:</strong> Continental, Oceanic, Hybrid.</li>
    <li><strong>Boundary Types:</strong> Divergent (constructive), Convergent (destructive), Transform (conservative).</li>
    <li><strong>Key Landforms:</strong> Mid‑Ocean Ridges, Trenches, Island Arcs, Fold Mountains, Fault Zones.</li>
    <li><strong>Sea‑Floor Spreading:</strong> New crust at ridges → symmetric magnetic stripes → age increases outward.</li>
    <li><strong>Driving Forces:</strong> Slab Pull > Ridge Push > Mantle Drag.</li>
    <li><strong>Important Numbers:</strong> Mid‑Atlantic spreading ≈ <span style='color: var(--warning);'>2.5 cm/yr</span>; Pacific Plate motion ≈ <span style='color: var(--warning);'>7 cm/yr</span>; Oldest oceanic crust ≈ <span style='color: var(--warning);'>200 Ma</span>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>9. Suggested Further Reading (Self‑Contained)</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>“Plate Tectonics” – J. H. Duncan, 2020 (Chapter 3: Boundaries).</li>
    <li>“Geodynamics” – Turcotte & Schubert, 2014 – sections on mantle convection.</li>
    <li>NCERT Class 12 – Chapter “Plate Tectonics” – concise definitions and diagrams.</li>
  </ol>
</div>
`;

