window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["ai-gen-physics-scalars-vectors-types-addition-resolution-dot-cross-products"] = `
<div class="revision-card">
    <h1>Scalars &amp; Vectors – Types, Addition, Resolution, Dot &amp; Cross Products</h1>

    <h2>1. Quick Definitions</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Scalar</strong>: Quantity having only magnitude (e.g., mass, temperature, time).</li>
        <li><strong>Vector</strong>: Quantity having both magnitude and direction (e.g., displacement, velocity, force).</li>
        <li><strong>Resultant</strong>: The single vector obtained after combining two or more vectors.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>2. Types of Scalars &amp; Vectors</h2>
    <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%;">
        <tr style="background:#f2f2f2;">
            <th>Category</th>
            <th>Examples</th>
            <th>Key Features</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Fundamental Scalars</strong></td>
            <td>Mass (m), Time (t), Temperature (T), Energy (E)</td>
            <td>Direction‑independent, additive algebraically.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Derived Scalars</strong></td>
            <td>Speed (v), Power (P), Pressure (p)</td>
            <td>Often obtained from vector magnitudes (e.g., speed = |velocity|).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Fundamental Vectors</strong></td>
            <td>Displacement (→s), Velocity (→v), Acceleration (→a), Force (→F)</td>
            <td>Represented by arrows; obey vector algebra.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Derived Vectors</strong></td>
            <td>Momentum (→p = m→v), Weight (→W = mg→j), Torque (→τ = →r × →F)</td>
            <td>Formed by multiplication of scalars with vectors or by vector products.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h2>3. Vector Addition</h2>
    <h3>3.1 Graphical Methods</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Tip‑and‑Tail (Polygon) Method</strong>: Place the tail of the second vector at the tip of the first; the resultant is drawn from the tail of the first to the tip of the last.</li>
        <li><strong>Parallelogram Method</strong>: Draw both vectors from a common origin, complete the parallelogram; the diagonal through the origin is the resultant.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3.2 Algebraic (Component) Method</h3>
    <p>For vectors <span style='color: var(--warning);'>→A</span> and <span style='color: var(--warning);'>→B</span> with components (A<sub>x</sub>, A<sub>y</sub>) and (B<sub>x</sub>, B<sub>y</sub>):</p>
    <pre style="background:#f9f9f9; padding:10px;">
<span style='color: var(--warning);'>→R = →A + →B = (A_x + B_x) î + (A_y + B_y) ĵ</span>
    </pre>
    <p>Magnitude: <span style='color: var(--warning);'>|R| = √[(A_x + B_x)² + (A_y + B_y)²]</span></p>

    <h2>4. Resolution of Vectors</h2>
    <p>Any vector can be broken into perpendicular components along chosen axes (usually x‑ and y‑axes).</p>
    <ul>
        <li>For a vector <span style='color: var(--warning);'>→V</span> making an angle <span style='color: var(--warning);'>θ</span> with the positive x‑axis:
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Horizontal component</strong>: V<sub>x</sub> = V cos<span style='color: var(--warning);'>θ</span></li>
                <li><strong>Vertical component</strong>: V<sub>y</sub> = V sin<span style='color: var(--warning);'>θ</span></li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li>When the angle is given with the y‑axis, swap sin and cos accordingly.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Mnemonic for Quick Recall</h3>
    <p><strong>“C‑S” – Cosine for X, Sine for Y</strong>. Remember: <em>Cosine goes with the adjacent side (x‑axis), Sine with the opposite side (y‑axis).</em></p>

    <h2>5. Dot (Scalar) Product</h2>
    <p>Definition: <span style='color: var(--warning);'>→A·→B = |A||B|cosθ</span>, where θ is the angle between the vectors.</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Result is a <strong>scalar</strong>.</li>
        <li>If θ = 90°, <span style='color: var(--warning);'>→A·→B = 0</span> (vectors are perpendicular).</li>
        <li>Component form: <span style='color: var(--warning);'>→A·→B = A_x B_x + A_y B_y + A_z B_z</span></li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Common Uses in NDA/CDS</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Work done: <span style='color: var(--warning);'>W = →F·→s</span></li>
        <li>Power: <span style='color: var(--warning);'>P = →F·→v</span></li>
        <li>Projection of A on B: <span style='color: var(--warning);'>proj_B A = (→A·→B)/|B|</span></li>
    </ul>

    <h2>6. Cross (Vector) Product</h2>
    <p>Definition: <span style='color: var(--warning);'>→A × →B = |A||B| sinθ  n̂</span>, where n̂ is a unit vector perpendicular to the plane of A and B (right‑hand rule).</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Result is a <strong>vector</strong> perpendicular to both A and B.</li>
        <li>Magnitude: <span style='color: var(--warning);'>|A×B| = |A||B| sinθ</span></li>
        <li>Component (determinant) form (for 3‑D):
            <pre style="background:#f9f9f9; padding:10px;">
| î   ĵ   k̂ |
| A_x A_y A_z |
| B_x B_y B_z |
            </pre>
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Mnemonic for Direction</h3>
    <p><strong>“Fingers‑Curl‑Thumb”</strong> – Point the fingers of your right hand along →A, curl them toward →B; the thumb points in the direction of →A × →B.</p>

    <h3>Key Applications</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Torque: <span style='color: var(--warning);'>→τ = →r × →F</span></li>
        <li>Magnetic Force on a moving charge: <span style='color: var(--warning);'>→F = q →v × →B</span></li>
        <li>Angular Momentum: <span style='color: var(--warning);'>→L = →r × →p</span></li>
    </ul>

    <h2>7. Summary of Important Formulas</h2>
    <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; background:#fafafa;">
        <tr style="background:#e0e0e0;">
            <th>Concept</th>
            <th>Formula</th>
            <th>Notes</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Vector Magnitude</td>
            <td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>|→A| = √(A_x² + A_y² + A_z²)</span></td>
            <td>Use Pythagoras in 2‑D/3‑D.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Resultant of Two Vectors</td>
            <td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>|R| = √(A² + B² + 2AB cosθ)</span></td>
            <td>Law of Cosines – handy for non‑orthogonal vectors.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Dot Product</td>
            <td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>→A·→B = A_x B_x + A_y B_y + A_z B_z = |A||B|cosθ</span></td>
            <td>Gives work, projection, angle.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Cross Product</td>
            <td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>→A × →B = (A_y B_z - A_z B_y) î + (A_z B_x - A_x B_z) ĵ + (A_x B_y - A_y B_x) k̂</span></td>
            <td>Right‑hand rule for direction.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Component Resolution</td>
            <td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>V_x = V cosθ, V_y = V sinθ</span></td>
            <td>Use when breaking forces or velocities.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Work Done</td>
            <td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>W = →F·→s = F s cosθ</span></td>
            <td>Zero if force ⟂ displacement.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Torque</td>
            <td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>τ = rF sinθ = |→r × →F|</span></td>
            <td>Maximum when r ⟂ F.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h2>8. Common Mistakes to Avoid (Exam Tips)</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Sign Errors</strong>: Always keep track of direction (+ve for right/up, –ve for left/down) when writing components.</li>
        <li><strong>Angle Reference</strong>: Verify whether the given angle is with the x‑axis or y‑axis; swapping sin and cos is a frequent slip.</li>
        <li><strong>Right‑Hand Rule</strong>: Practice the thumb‑finger method for cross product; draw a quick sketch to avoid confusion.</li>
        <li><strong>Units</strong>: Scalars and vectors share the same units (e.g., N·m for torque, J for work). Do not mix up N·m (torque) with J (energy) in answer sheets.</li>
        <li><strong>Zero Dot Product</strong>: Remember that a zero dot product indicates perpendicular vectors, not zero magnitude.</li>
        <li><strong>Magnitude vs. Component</strong>: When a question asks for “magnitude of the resultant”, do not give the component value.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>9. Quick Revision Checklist (One‑Minute Recall)</h2>
    <ol>
        <li>Identify if the quantity is scalar or vector.</li>
        <li>Write vectors in component form (î, ĵ, k̂).</li>
        <li>For addition – add like components.</li>
        <li>For resolution – use <strong>C‑S</strong> mnemonic.</li>
        <li>Dot product → check cosθ, scalar result.</li>
        <li>Cross product → check sinθ, right‑hand rule, vector result.</li>
        <li>Plug into formula table for work, torque, power.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>10. Final Exam‑Ready Tip</h2>
    <p>In the NDA/CDS/AFCAT physics section, the <strong>most common 2‑mark question</strong> is a direct application of the dot product (work) or cross product (torque). Memorise the compact forms:</p>
    <pre style="background:#f0f8ff; padding:10px;">
Work: W = F s cosθ   Torque: τ = r F sinθ
    </pre>
    <p>Write the answer in the required unit (J for work, N·m for torque) and always state the direction for torque (e.g., “out of the page”). This precision fetches the extra mark for clarity.</p>
</div>
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["ai-gen-physics-elasticity-hooke-s-law-moduli-of-elasticity-stress-strain"] = `
<div class="revision-card">
  <h1>Elasticity – Hooke’s Law, Moduli of Elasticity & Stress‑Strain</h1>

  <h2>1. What is Elasticity?</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Elasticity</strong> is the property of a material to regain its original shape and size after the removal of an external force that caused deformation. The deformation is <em>reversible</em> as long as the applied stress does not exceed the material’s elastic limit.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Hooke’s Law</h2>
  <p><strong>Hooke’s Law</strong> states that, within the elastic region, the restoring force (or stress) is directly proportional to the deformation (or strain).</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Mathematical form (force version): <strong>F = -k x</strong></li>
    <li>Mathematical form (stress‑strain version): <strong>σ = E ε</strong></li>
    <li>Where:
      <ul>
        <li><strong>F</strong> = restoring force (N)</li>
        <li><strong>k</strong> = spring constant (N m⁻¹)</li>
        <li><strong>x</strong> = extension/compression (m)</li>
        <li><strong>σ</strong> = stress (Pa)</li>
        <li><strong>E</strong> = Young’s modulus (Pa)</li>
        <li><strong>ε</strong> = strain (dimensionless)</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>Valid only up to the <strong>proportional limit</strong> (≈ <span style='color: var(--warning);'>0.2 % strain</span> for most metals).</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Mnemonic for Hooke’s Law</h3>
  <p><strong>F = –k x</strong> → “<em>Force equals negative spring‑constant times extension</em>”. Remember the minus sign indicates the force is opposite to the direction of displacement.</p>

  <h2>3. Stress and Strain – Definitions</h2>
  <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse: collapse; width:100%;">
    <tr style="background:#f2f2f2;">
      <th>Term</th>
      <th>Symbol</th>
      <th>Formula</th>
      <th>Units</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Stress (Normal)</strong></td>
      <td>σ</td>
      <td>σ = F/A</td>
      <td>Pascal (Pa) = N m⁻²</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Shear Stress</strong></td>
      <td>τ</td>
      <td>τ = F<sub>parallel</sub>/A</td>
      <td>Pa</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Strain (Normal)</strong></td>
      <td>ε</td>
      <td>ε = ΔL/L₀</td>
      <td>Dimensionless (often expressed in %)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Shear Strain</strong></td>
      <td>γ</td>
      <td>γ = Δx/h</td>
      <td>Dimensionless</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h2>4. Moduli of Elasticity</h2>
  <p>Moduli quantify a material’s resistance to different types of deformation. The three primary moduli are:</p>

  <h3>4.1 Young’s Modulus (E) – Tensile/Compressive</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Definition: Ratio of normal stress to normal strain in the linear elastic region.</li>
    <li>Formula: <strong>E = σ/ε = (F/A) / (ΔL/L₀)</strong></li>
    <li>Units: <strong>Pa (N m⁻²)</strong></li>
    <li>Typical values:
      <ul>
        <li>Steel: <span style='color: var(--warning);'>200 GPa</span></li>
        <li>Aluminium: <span style='color: var(--warning);'>70 GPa</span></li>
        <li>Rubber (elastic region): <span style='color: var(--warning);'>0.01–0.1 GPa</span></li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>4.2 Bulk Modulus (K) – Volumetric</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Definition: Ratio of hydrostatic pressure increase to relative volume decrease.</li>
    <li>Formula: <strong>K = -ΔP / (ΔV/V₀)</strong></li>
    <li>Units: Pa</li>
    <li>Typical values:
      <ul>
        <li>Water: <span style='color: var(--warning);'>2.2 GPa</span></li>
        <li>Steel: <span style='color: var(--warning);'>160 GPa</span></li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>4.3 Shear Modulus (G) – Modulus of Rigidity</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Definition: Ratio of shear stress to shear strain.</li>
    <li>Formula: <strong>G = τ/γ = (F<sub>parallel</sub>/A) / (Δx/h)</strong></li>
    <li>Units: Pa</li>
    <li>Typical values:
      <ul>
        <li>Steel: <span style='color: var(--warning);'>80 GPa</span></li>
        <li>Aluminium: <span style='color: var(--warning);'>26 GPa</span></li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Mnemonic for the Three Moduli</h3>
  <p>Remember <strong>“Y B S – Young, Bulk, Shear”</strong>. The order also follows the type of deformation: <em>Length → Volume → Shape</em>.</p>

  <h2>5. Stress‑Strain Curve – Key Points</h2>
  <p>The typical engineering stress‑strain diagram for a ductile metal is shown below (values are illustrative).</p>
  <img src="https://i.imgur.com/StressStrainDiagram.png" alt="Stress-Strain Curve" style="max-width:100%; height:auto;">

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Proportional Limit</strong> – Linear region where Hooke’s law holds.</li>
    <li><strong>Elastic Limit (Yield Point)</strong> – Maximum stress that can be applied without permanent deformation.</li>
    <li><strong>Yield Strength (σ<sub>y</sub>)</strong> – Stress at which plastic deformation begins (often taken at 0.2 % offset strain).</li>
    <li><strong>Ultimate Tensile Strength (UTS)</strong> – Highest stress the material can sustain.</li>
    <li><strong>Fracture Point</strong> – Stress at which the specimen breaks.</li>
    <li><strong>Modulus of Resilience</strong> – Area under the elastic portion; energy per unit volume stored.</li>
    <li><strong>Modulus of Toughness</strong> – Total area under the curve; energy per unit volume absorbed before fracture.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Quick Exam Tip</h3>
  <p>When a question asks for “energy absorbed up to the elastic limit”, calculate <strong>(½ E ε²)</strong> per unit volume or use the triangular area <strong>(½ σ<sub>y</sub> ε<sub>y</sub>)</strong>.</p>

  <h2>6. Comparative Table – Common Materials</h2>
  <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse: collapse; width:100%; text-align:center;">
    <tr style="background:#e0e0e0;">
      <th>Material</th>
      <th>Young’s Modulus (E)</th>
      <th>Bulk Modulus (K)</th>
      <th>Shear Modulus (G)</th>
      <th>Typical Yield Strength (σ<sub>y</sub>)</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Steel (Mild)</td>
      <td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>200 GPa</span></td>
      <td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>160 GPa</span></td>
      <td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>80 GPa</span></td>
      <td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>250 MPa</span></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Aluminium</td>
      <td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>70 GPa</span></td>
      <td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>76 GPa</span></td>
      <td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>26 GPa</span></td>
      <td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>95 MPa</span></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Copper</td>
      <td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>110 GPa</span></td>
      <td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>140 GPa</span></td>
      <td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>44 GPa</span></td>
      <td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>210 MPa</span></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Rubber (elastic region)</td>
      <td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>0.01–0.1 GPa</span></td>
      <td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>2 MPa</span></td>
      <td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>0.001–0.01 GPa</span></td>
      <td>—</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h2>7. Important Formulas – Quick Reference</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Stress (σ) = F/A</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Strain (ε) = ΔL/L₀</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Young’s Modulus (E) = σ/ε</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Bulk Modulus (K) = -ΔP / (ΔV/V₀)</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Shear Modulus (G) = τ/γ</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Relation between Moduli (for isotropic materials):</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>E = 2G(1 + ν)</li>
        <li>K = E / [3(1 - 2ν)]</li>
        <li>Where ν = Poisson’s ratio (typical 0.3 for metals)</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Energy stored per unit volume (elastic region):</strong> U = ½ σ ε = ½ E ε²</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>8. Common Mistakes & How to Avoid Them</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Confusing stress with pressure.</strong> Stress is internal force per unit area; pressure is external normal force per unit area on a surface.</li>
    <li><strong>Using total elongation instead of strain.</strong> Always divide ΔL by original length L₀.</li>
    <li><strong>Neglecting sign convention.</strong> Hooke’s law includes a negative sign because the restoring force opposes displacement.</li>
    <li><strong>Mixing up bulk and shear modulus.</strong> Bulk deals with volume change under hydrostatic pressure; shear deals with shape change under parallel forces.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>9. Quick Revision Checklist (Exam‑Ready)</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Define <strong>stress</strong> and <strong>strain</strong> with symbols σ and ε.</li>
    <li>State Hooke’s law in both force and stress‑strain forms.</li>
    <li>Recall the three moduli (E, K, G) and their formulas.</li>
    <li>Memorise typical values for steel, aluminium, copper, and rubber (use the <em>Y B S</em> mnemonic).</li>
    <li>Identify points on the stress‑strain curve: proportional limit, elastic limit, yield strength, UTS, fracture.</li>
    <li>Be able to compute energy per unit volume up to the elastic limit.</li>
    <li>Remember the inter‑relation formulas involving Poisson’s ratio (ν).</li>
    <li>Practice unit conversions: 1 GPa = <span style='color: var(--warning);'>10⁹ Pa</span>, 1 MPa = <span style='color: var(--warning);'>10⁶ Pa</span>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>10. Final Exam Tip</h2>
  <p>When a question provides a stress‑strain graph but no numerical values, you can estimate <strong>E</strong> from the slope of the initial linear portion, and read off <strong>σ<sub>y</sub></strong> and <strong>UTS</strong> directly from the graph. Always check whether the problem asks for “engineering stress” (load/A₀) or “true stress” (load/A<sub>instant</sub>) – NDA/CDS usually expects engineering stress.</p>
</div>
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["ai-gen-physics-optical-instruments-microscope-telescope-human-eye-defects"] = `
<div class="revision-card">
    <h1 style="text-align:center;">Optical Instruments – Quick Revision</h1>

    <h2>1. Compound Microscope</h2>
    <p><strong>Purpose:</strong> To view objects that are too small for the naked eye by producing a highly enlarged real image.</p>

    <h3>Construction & Working</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Objective lens</strong> – Low‑power, short focal length (<span style='color: var(--warning);'>f_o</span>), placed close to the specimen.</li>
        <li><strong>Eyepiece (ocular) lens</strong> – High‑power, long focal length (<span style='color: var(--warning);'>f_e</span>), acts as a simple magnifier.</li>
        <li>The objective forms a real, inverted image at its focal plane; the eyepiece then magnifies this image.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Key Formulas</h3>
    <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse;">
        <tr style="background:#f2f2f2;">
            <th>Quantity</th><th>Expression</th><th>Notes</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Magnification of Objective (M_o)</strong></td>
            <td>\\(M_o = \\dfrac{v_o}{u_o}\\)  (≈ \\(\\dfrac{L}{f_o}\\) where <span style='color: var(--warning);'>L = 25 cm</span>)</td>
            <td>v_o = image distance, u_o = object distance</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Magnification of Eyepiece (M_e)</strong></td>
            <td>\\(M_e = \\dfrac{25\\text{ cm}}{f_e}\\)</td>
            <td>Standard near‑point distance = <span style='color: var(--warning);'>25 cm</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Total Magnification (M)</strong></td>
            <td>\\(M = M_o \\times M_e = \\dfrac{25\\text{ cm}}{f_e}\\times\\dfrac{L}{f_o}\\)</td>
            <td>For a normal microscope, <span style='color: var(--warning);'>L ≈ 160 mm</span></td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h3>Important Points</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Image formed by the objective must lie within the focal length of the eyepiece for a virtual, enlarged final image.</li>
        <li>Resolution limit is given by Rayleigh’s criterion: \\(\\theta_{min}=1.22\\lambda/D\\).</li>
        <li>Mnemonic for total magnification: <em>“25 over f_e times L over f_o” → 25 f_o / f_e L</em>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>2. Telescope</h2>
    <p><strong>Purpose:</strong> To view distant objects (stars, planets) by producing an image at infinity.</p>

    <h3>Types</h3>
    <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse;">
        <tr style="background:#f2f2f2;">
            <th>Type</th><th>Configuration</th><th>Key Feature</th><th>Typical Use</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Refracting (Astronomical)</strong></td>
            <td>Objective (convex) + Eyepiece (convex)</td>
            <td>Angular magnification \\(M = \\dfrac{f_o}{f_e}\\)</td>
            <td>Star‑gazing, terrestrial viewing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Reflecting (Newtonian)</strong></td>
            <td>Primary concave mirror + Flat diagonal secondary</td>
            <td>Magnification \\(M = \\dfrac{f_{primary}}{f_e}\\)</td>
            <td>Large‑aperture astronomy</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Compound (Keplerian)</strong></td>
            <td>Two convex lenses, image formed at infinity</td>
            <td>Same formula as refracting, but longer focal lengths</td>
            <td>High‑power astronomical telescopes</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h3>Key Formulas</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Angular Magnification (M)</strong> – \\(M = \\dfrac{f_o}{f_e}\\) for astronomical telescopes (image at infinity).</li>
        <li><strong>Focal Length Relation</strong> – For a normal adjustment, the final image is at infinity, so the separation between lenses ≈ \\(f_o + f_e\\).</li>
        <li><strong>Exit Pupil</strong> – Diameter = \\(\\dfrac{D}{M}\\) where <span style='color: var(--warning);'>D</span> = objective aperture.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Historical Nuggets</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>1609</strong> – <em>Galileo</em> built the first practical refracting telescope (<span style='color: var(--warning);'>f_o ≈ 1 m, f_e ≈ 25 mm</span>).</li>
        <li><strong>1668</strong> – <em>Isaac Newton</em> invented the reflecting telescope, eliminating chromatic aberration.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>3. Human Eye – Optical Model</h2>
    <p>The eye works as a variable‑power optical instrument that forms a real image on the retina.</p>

    <h3>Components & Their Powers</h3>
    <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse;">
        <tr style="background:#f2f2f2;">
            <th>Part</th><th>Function</th><th>Typical Power (Dioptres)</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Cornea</strong></td><td>Fixed refracting surface</td><td>≈ <span style='color: var(--warning);'>+43 D</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Lens</strong></td><td>Accommodation (changes curvature)</td><td>≈ <span style='color: var(--warning);'>+19 D</span> (relaxed) to <span style='color: var(--warning);'>+22 D</span> (accommodated)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Aqueous & Vitreous Humour</strong></td><td>Medium with refractive index ≈1.33</td><td>≈ <span style='color: var(--warning);'>+1 D</span> each</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h3>Accommodation</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Near objects → Ciliary muscle contracts → Lens becomes more convex → Power ↑.</li>
        <li>Far objects → Ciliary muscle relaxes → Lens flattens → Power ↓.</li>
        <li>Maximum accommodation in a young adult ≈ <span style='color: var(--warning);'>+15 D</span> (≈ 6 cm near point).</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Common Defects & Corrections</h3>
    <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse;">
        <tr style="background:#f2f2f2;">
            <th>Defect</th><th>Cause</th><th>Lens Needed</th><th>Mnemonic</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Myopia (Near‑sighted)</strong></td>
            <td>Eye’s total power > required; image forms in front of retina.</td>
            <td>Concave (diverging) lens, power = –<span style='color: var(--warning);'>D</span></td>
            <td>“My‑Optic = My = Near” → use <strong>Minus</strong> lens.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Hypermetropia (Far‑sighted)</strong></td>
            <td>Eye’s power insufficient; image forms behind retina.</td>
            <td>Convex (converging) lens, power = +<span style='color: var(--warning);'>D</span></td>
            <td>“Hyper = High‑power needed → Plus lens.”</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Astigmatism</strong></td>
            <td>Cornea or lens has different curvatures in meridians.</td>
            <td>Cylindrical lens (axis specified)</td>
            <td>“A‑stigmata → Axis needed.”</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Presbyopia</strong></td>
            <td>Loss of accommodation with age (≈ <span style='color: var(--warning);'>40 yr</span>).</td>
            <td>Reading (convex) lens, usually +<span style='color: var(--warning);'>2 D to +3 D</span>.</td>
            <td>“Presby‑old → Add power.”</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h3>Exam Tip</h3>
    <p>When a question asks for the “power of corrective lens”, always use the sign convention: <strong>negative for myopia, positive for hypermetropia and presbyopia**. Remember the formula <em>Power (P) = 1/f (in metres)</em> and convert dioptres directly.</p>

    <h2>4. Comparison – Microscope vs Telescope vs Eye</h2>
    <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse;">
        <tr style="background:#e0e0e0;">
            <th>Feature</th><th>Microscope</th><th>Telescope</th><th>Human Eye</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Object Distance</strong></td>
            <td>Very small, placed just beyond focal point of objective.</td>
            <td>Practically infinite.</td>
            <td>Variable (near point ≈ <span style='color: var(--warning);'>25 cm</span> to infinity).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Final Image Position</strong></td>
            <td>At near point (virtual, enlarged).</td>
            <td>At infinity (parallel rays).</td>
            <td>On retina (real, inverted).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Magnification Formula</strong></td>
            <td>\\(M = \\dfrac{25}{f_e}\\times\\dfrac{L}{f_o}\\)</td>
            <td>\\(M = \\dfrac{f_o}{f_e}\\)</td>
            <td>\\(M = \\dfrac{25}{\\text{near point distance}}\\) (≈ 1 for relaxed eye).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Key Aberration</strong></td>
            <td>Chromatic & spherical (mitigated by achromats).</td>
            <td>Chromatic (refracting) / coma (reflecting).</td>
            <td>Myopia, hypermetropia, astigmatism, presbyopia.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h2>5. Quick Mnemonics & Tricks for NDA/CDS/AFCAT</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>“C O R N E = 43 D”</strong> – Remember corneal power.</li>
        <li><strong>“M‑H‑A‑P”</strong> – Myopia, Hypermetropia, Astigmatism, Presbyopia – the four common defects.</li>
        <li><strong>“FOE = 25/f_e”</strong> – <em>FOE</em> stands for <strong>F</strong>ocal length of <strong>O</strong>cular (eyepiece) → Magnification of eyepiece.</li>
        <li><strong>“G‑N‑T”</strong> – <em>G</em>alileo (1609), <em>N</em>ewton (1668), <em>T</em>elescope – chronological order of major inventions.</li>
        <li>For any lens problem, write down the sign convention first: <br>
            <strong>Convex = +, Concave = –, Object distance (u) = – (real), Image distance (v) = + (real),</strong> <br>
            then apply \\( \\frac{1}{f} = \\frac{1}{v} - \\frac{1}{u}\\).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>6. Frequently Asked Exam Questions (with short answers)</h2>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Q:</strong> A microscope has an objective of focal length 4 mm and an eyepiece of focal length 25 mm. Compute its total magnification (assume normal adjustment).<br>
            <strong>A:</strong> \\(M = \\dfrac{25}{f_e}\\times\\dfrac{L}{f_o} = \\dfrac{25}{25}\\times\\dfrac{160}{4}=1\\times40= \\mathbf{40×}\\).</li>
        <li><strong>Q:</strong> What is the angular magnification of a telescope with objective focal length 1000 mm and eyepiece focal length 20 mm?<br>
            <strong>A:</strong> \\(M = \\dfrac{f_o}{f_e}= \\dfrac{1000}{20}= \\mathbf{50×}\\).</li>
        <li><strong>Q:</strong> A myopic person sees clearly at 30 cm. What is the power of the required corrective lens?<br>
            <strong>A:</strong> Lens must form image at infinity for object at 30 cm: \\(P = -\\dfrac{1}{0.30}= -\\mathbf{3.33 D}\\).</li>
        <li><strong>Q:</strong> State the main advantage of a reflecting telescope over a refracting one.<br>
            <strong>A:</strong> No chromatic aberration because mirrors reflect all wavelengths equally.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>7. Final Exam‑Ready Checklist</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>✔️ Memorise focal lengths and powers of cornea, lens, and typical instrument lenses.</li>
        <li>✔️ Be able to derive and use the three key magnification formulas.</li>
        <li>✔️ Remember sign conventions for lens formula and power.</li>
        <li>✔️ Know the historical years (<span style='color: var(--warning);'>1609, 1668, 1665</span>) for quick factual questions.</li>
        <li>✔️ Practice at least five numerical problems each for microscope and telescope before the exam.</li>
        <li>✔️ Review the defect table; be ready to write the corrective lens power in dioptres instantly.</li>
    </ul>

    <p style="text-align:center; font-weight:bold;">Good luck! Stay focused and revise these core concepts daily.</p>
</div>
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["ai-gen-chemistry-matter-states-properties-changes-classification"] = `
<div class="revision-card">
    <h1>Physical Chemistry – Matter: States, Properties, Changes & Classification</h1>

    <h2>1. What is Matter?</h2>
    <p><strong>Matter</strong> is anything that occupies space and has mass. It exists in distinct <strong>states</strong> and can undergo <strong>physical</strong> or <strong>chemical</strong> changes.</p>

    <h2>2. Classification of Matter</h2>
    <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%;">
        <tr style="background:#f2f2f2;">
            <th>Category</th>
            <th>Sub‑categories</th>
            <th>Key Features</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Pure Substances</strong></td>
            <td>Elements, Compounds</td>
            <td>
                <ul>
                    <li>Fixed composition</li>
                    <li>Definite chemical formula</li>
                    <li>Same physical & chemical properties throughout</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Mixtures</strong></td>
            <td>Homogeneous (Solutions) & Heterogeneous (Colloids, Suspensions)</td>
            <td>
                <ul>
                    <li>Variable composition</li>
                    <li>Components retain individual identities</li>
                    <li>Separated by physical methods</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h3>Mnemonic for Classification</h3>
    <p>Remember <strong>“Pure Mixes”</strong> → <strong>P</strong>ure (Elements & Compounds) and <strong>M</strong>ixtures (Homogeneous & Heterogeneous).</p>

    <h2>3. States of Matter</h2>
    <p>The classic four states are remembered by the mnemonic <strong>“S L G P”</strong> (Solid, Liquid, Gas, Plasma). A fifth state, <strong>Bose‑Einstein Condensate (BEC)</strong>, appears at ultra‑low temperatures.</p>

    <h3>3.1 Comparative Table of the Four Classical States</h3>
    <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; margin-top:10px;">
        <tr style="background:#e6e6e6;">
            <th>Property</th>
            <th>Solid</th>
            <th>Liquid</th>
            <th>Gas</th>
            <th>Plasma</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Shape</strong></td>
            <td>Definite</td>
            <td>Indefinite (takes container’s shape)</td>
            <td>Indefinite</td>
            <td>Indefinite</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Volume</strong></td>
            <td>Definite</td>
            <td>Definite</td>
            <td>Indefinite</td>
            <td>Indefinite</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Particle Arrangement</strong></td>
            <td>Close‑packed, fixed positions</td>
            <td>Close but mobile</td>
            <td>Widely spaced, random motion</td>
            <td>Ionised, free electrons & nuclei</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Inter‑molecular Forces (IMF)</strong></td>
            <td>Strong (ionic, covalent, metallic, network)</td>
            <td>Moderate (hydrogen bonding, dipole‑dipole, London)</td>
            <td>Weak (primarily London dispersion)</td>
            <td>Negligible (electrostatic repulsion dominates)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Compressibility</strong></td>
            <td>Very low</td>
            <td>Low</td>
            <td>High</td>
            <td>Very high</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Typical Examples</strong></td>
            <td>Ice, NaCl, Diamond</td>
            <td>Water, Ethanol, Mercury</td>
            <td>O₂, CO₂, Steam</td>
            <td>Fluorescent lamp, Sun’s corona</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h3>3.2 Phase Change Equations (Exam Essentials)</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Melting (Fusion)</strong>: <strong>q = m·L<sub>f</sub></strong></li>
        <li><strong>Freezing</strong>: <strong>q = m·L<sub>f</sub></strong> (heat released)</li>
        <li><strong>Vaporisation</strong>: <strong>q = m·L<sub>v</sub></strong></li>
        <li><strong>Condensation</strong>: <strong>q = m·L<sub>v</sub></strong> (heat released)</li>
        <li><strong>Sublimation</strong>: <strong>q = m·L<sub>s</sub></strong></li>
    </ul>
    <p>Where <strong>L<sub>f</sub></strong>, <strong>L<sub>v</sub></strong>, <strong>L<sub>s</sub></strong> are latent heats of fusion, vaporisation, and sublimation respectively (units: J kg⁻¹).</p>

    <h3>3.3 Critical Temperature & Pressure (Important Numbers)</h3>
    <p>For water: <span style='color: var(--warning);'>Critical Temperature = <span style='color: var(--warning);'>374 °C</span></span>, <span style='color: var(--warning);'>Critical Pressure = <span style='color: var(--warning);'>22.06 MPa</span></span>. Remember the mnemonic “<strong>3‑7‑4 °C, 22 MPa</strong>”.</p>

    <h2>4. Physical vs Chemical Properties</h2>
    <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; margin-top:10px;">
        <tr style="background:#f9f9f9;">
            <th>Aspect</th>
            <th>Physical Property</th>
            <th>Chemical Property</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Definition</strong></td>
            <td>Observed without changing composition</td>
            <td>Observed when composition changes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Examples</strong></td>
            <td>Melting point, density, colour, conductivity</td>
            <td>Reactivity with acid, oxidation‑reduction, combustibility</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Measurement</strong></td>
            <td>Simple instruments (thermometer, balance)</td>
            <td>Often requires reagents & observation of products</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h2>5. Physical vs Chemical Changes</h2>
    <p>Both are frequently asked in NDA/CDS objective sections. Keep the following table handy.</p>
    <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; margin-top:10px;">
        <tr style="background:#e0e0e0;">
            <th>Change Type</th>
            <th>Key Indicators</th>
            <th>Examples</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Physical Change</strong></td>
            <td>Change of state, shape, size; no new substance formed</td>
            <td>Ice melting, evaporation, grinding sugar</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Chemical Change</strong></td>
            <td>Colour change, gas evolution, precipitate formation, temperature change (exothermic/endothermic)</td>
            <td>Iron rusting, burning of paper, reaction of Na with H₂O</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h3>Mnemonic for Chemical Change Indicators</h3>
    <p><strong>“C‑G‑P‑T”</strong> – <strong>C</strong>olour, <strong>G</strong>as, <strong>P</strong>recipitate, <strong>T</strong>emperature change.</p>

    <h2>6. Detailed Look at Solids</h2>
    <h3>6.1 Crystalline vs Amorphous Solids</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Crystalline</strong>: Long‑range order, definite melting point, e.g., NaCl, quartz.</li>
        <li><strong>Amorphous</strong>: No long‑range order, softening range instead of sharp melting point, e.g., glass, wax.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>6.2 Types of Crystalline Solids (Exam‑friendly)</h3>
    <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; margin-top:10px;">
        <tr style="background:#d9edf7;">
            <th>Type</th>
            <th>Bonding</th>
            <th>Examples</th>
            <th>Key Property</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Ionic</strong></td>
            <td>Electrostatic attraction between cations & anions</td>
            <td>NaCl, MgO</td>
            <td>High melting point, brittle</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Covalent (Network)</strong></td>
            <td>Strong covalent bonds in a 3‑D network</td>
            <td>Diamond, SiO₂</td>
            <td>Very high melting point, hard</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Metallic</strong></td>
            <td>Delocalised electron sea</td>
            <td>Cu, Fe</td>
            <td>Good conductivity, ductile</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Molecular</strong></td>
            <td>Weak van der Waals or hydrogen bonds</td>
            <td>Ice (H₂O), dry ice (CO₂)</td>
            <td>Low melting point, volatile</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h2>7. Gaseous Laws – Must‑Know Formulas</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Ideal Gas Equation</strong>: <strong>PV = nRT</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Boyle’s Law (T constant)</strong>: <strong>P₁V₁ = P₂V₂</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Charles’s Law (P constant)</strong>: <strong>V₁/T₁ = V₂/T₂</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Avogadro’s Law (P,T constant)</strong>: <strong>V₁/n₁ = V₂/n₂</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Combined Gas Law</strong>: <strong>(P₁V₁)/T₁ = (P₂V₂)/T₂</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Dalton’s Law of Partial Pressures</strong>: <strong>P<sub>total</sub> = ΣP<sub>i</sub></strong></li>
    </ul>
    <p>Key constants for NDA: <span style='color: var(--warning);'>R = 0.0821 L·atm mol⁻¹ K⁻¹</span> or <span style='color: var(--warning);'>8.314 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹</span>.</p>

    <h3>Quick Calculation Tip</h3>
    <p>When converting temperature, always use <strong>T(K) = T(°C) + <span style='color: var(--warning);'>273</span></strong>. Remember the mnemonic “<strong>273 K = 0 °C</strong>”.</p>

    <h2>8. Classification of Changes – Summary Flowchart (Textual)</h2>
    <p><strong>Start → Change observed?</strong></p>
    <ol>
        <li>If <strong>no new substance</strong> → <strong>Physical Change</strong> (e.g., melting, sublimation).</li>
        <li>If <strong>new substance formed</strong> → <strong>Chemical Change</strong> (e.g., combustion, oxidation).</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Use the <strong>C‑G‑P‑T</strong> mnemonic to confirm a chemical change.</p>

    <h2>9. Frequently Asked Objective Questions (with Answers)</h2>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Which state has the highest kinetic energy of particles?</strong> – <strong>Plasma</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Latent heat of vaporisation of water is approximately:</strong> – <span style='color: var(--warning);'>2260 kJ kg⁻¹</span>.</li>
        <li><strong>At <span style='color: var(--warning);'>0 °C</span> and <span style='color: var(--warning);'>1 atm</span>, 1 mol of an ideal gas occupies:</strong> – <span style='color: var(--warning);'>22.4 L</span>.</li>
        <li><strong>Which of the following is an amorphous solid?</strong> – <strong>Glass</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Which law relates pressure and volume at constant temperature?</strong> – <strong>Boyle’s Law</strong>.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>10. Exam‑Ready Tips</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Underline key numbers</strong> (e.g., latent heats, critical values) during revision; they appear in MCQs.</li>
        <li>Use the <strong>“S L G P”</strong> and <strong>“C‑G‑P‑T”</strong> mnemonics to quickly eliminate wrong options.</li>
        <li>Remember that <strong>phase change equations</strong> involve only mass and latent heat – no temperature term.</li>
        <li>For gas‑law problems, always convert temperature to Kelvin and pressure to atm (or Pa) before plugging into formulas.</li>
        <li>When a question mentions “no change in composition”, think <strong>Physical Change</strong>; if new substances are mentioned, think <strong>Chemical Change</strong>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>11. Quick Reference Box (HTML Styled)</h2>
    <div style="border:2px solid #007bff; padding:10px; background:#e9f5ff; margin-top:10px;">
        <p><strong>Key Numbers</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>Melting point of ice: <span style='color: var(--warning);'>0 °C (273 K)</span></li>
            <li>Boiling point of water: <span style='color: var(--warning);'>100 °C (373 K)</span></li>
            <li>Latent heat of fusion (water): <span style='color: var(--warning);'>334 kJ kg⁻¹</span></li>
            <li>Latent heat of vaporisation (water): <span style='color: var(--warning);'>2260 kJ kg⁻¹</span></li>
            <li>Critical temperature of water: <span style='color: var(--warning);'>374 °C</span></li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>12. Concluding Summary</h2>
    <p>Understanding the <strong>classification of matter</strong>, distinguishing between <strong>physical</strong> and <strong>chemical</strong> properties, and mastering the <strong>state‑change equations</strong> are essential for any NDA/CDS/AFCAT Physical Chemistry paper. Use the tables, mnemonics, and tip‑lists above for rapid recall during the exam.</p>
</div>
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["ai-gen-chemistry-solutions-types-concentration-colligative-properties"] = `
<div class="revision-card">
    <h2>Physical Chemistry – Solutions</h2>
    
    <h3>1. What is a Solution?</h3>
    <p>A <strong>solution</strong> is a homogeneous mixture of two or more substances where the particles of the solute are uniformly distributed in the solvent at the molecular level.</p>
    
    <h3>2. Types of Solutions</h3>
    <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; margin-bottom:15px;">
        <tr style="background:#f2f2f2;">
            <th>Type</th>
            <th>Definition</th>
            <th>Examples</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Based on Physical State</strong></td>
            <td>Solid‑in‑solid, liquid‑in‑liquid, gas‑in‑gas, etc.</td>
            <td>Alloy (solid‑in‑solid), Salt water (liquid‑in‑liquid), Air (gas‑in‑gas)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Based on Solvent</strong></td>
            <td>Aqueous, non‑aqueous, gaseous</td>
            <td>Glucose solution (aqueous), Iodine in carbon tetrachloride (non‑aqueous)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Based on Concentration</strong></td>
            <td>Dilute, concentrated, saturated, supersaturated</td>
            <td>Saturated NaCl solution at 25 °C, Supersaturated sugar syrup</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Based on Electrolytic Nature</strong></td>
            <td>Electrolytes (strong, weak) and non‑electrolytes</td>
            <td>NaCl (strong electrolyte), Acetic acid (weak electrolyte), Sugar (non‑electrolyte)</td>
        </tr>
    </table>
    
    <h3>3. Concentration Terms – Formulas & Mnemonics</h3>
    <p>Remember the mnemonic <strong>“MOLAR BOP”</strong> for the most common concentration units:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>M</strong> – <strong>Molarity (M)</strong> = <span style='color: var(--warning);'>moles of solute</span> / <span style='color: var(--warning);'>L of solution</span></li>
        <li><strong>O</strong> – <strong>Molality (m)</strong> = <span style='color: var(--warning);'>moles of solute</span> / <span style='color: var(--warning);'>kg of solvent</span></li>
        <li><strong>L</strong> – <strong>Normality (N)</strong> = <span style='color: var(--warning);'>equivalents</span> / <span style='color: var(--warning);'>L of solution</span></li>
        <li><strong>A</strong> – <strong>Mass % (w/w%)</strong> = (mass of solute / mass of solution) × 100</li>
        <li><strong>R</strong> – <strong>Volume % (v/v%)</strong> = (volume of solute / volume of solution) × 100</li>
        <li><strong>B</strong> – <strong>Parts per million (ppm)</strong> = (mass of solute / mass of solution) × 10⁶</li>
        <li><strong>O</strong> – <strong>Parts per billion (ppb)</strong> = (mass of solute / mass of solution) × 10⁹</li>
        <li><strong>P</strong> – <strong>Mol fraction (X)</strong> = moles of component / total moles</li>
    </ul>
    
    <h4>Key Conversion Tips</h4>
    <ul>
        <li>For dilute aqueous solutions, <strong>M ≈ m</strong> because density ≈ 1 g mL⁻¹.</li>
        <li>Normality = M × <strong>n</strong> (where <strong>n</strong> = number of replaceable H⁺/OH⁻/e⁻ per molecule).</li>
        <li>To convert ppm to mg L⁻¹ in water, use 1 ppm = 1 mg L⁻¹ (density ≈ 1 g mL⁻¹).</li>
    </ul>
    
    <h3>4. Colligative Properties – The “BFPV” Rule</h3>
    <p>Colligative properties depend only on the number of solute particles, not on their nature. Use the mnemonic <strong>“BFPV”</strong> to recall them:</p>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>B</strong> – Boiling‑point elevation (ΔT_b)</li>
        <li><strong>F</strong> – Freezing‑point depression (ΔT_f)</li>
        <li><strong>P</strong> – Osmotic pressure (π)</li>
        <li><strong>V</strong> – Vapor‑pressure lowering (ΔP)</li>
    </ol>
    
    <h4>4.1 Boiling‑Point Elevation</h4>
    <p>Formula: <strong>ΔT_b = i·K_b·m</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>i</strong> – Van’t Hoff factor (number of particles formed per formula unit).</li>
        <li><strong>K_b</strong> – Ebullioscopic constant (°C kg mol⁻¹) – tabulated for each solvent.</li>
        <li><strong>m</strong> – Molality of the solution.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> For 0.5 m NaCl (i = 2) in water (K_b = <span style='color: var(--warning);'>0.512 °C kg mol⁻¹</span>), ΔT_b = 2 × 0.512 × 0.5 = <span style='color: var(--warning);'>0.512 °C</span>.</p>
    
    <h4>4.2 Freezing‑Point Depression</h4>
    <p>Formula: <strong>ΔT_f = i·K_f·m</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>K_f</strong> – Cryoscopic constant (°C kg mol⁻¹).</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Example:</strong> For 1.0 m glucose (i = 1) in water (K_f = <span style='color: var(--warning);'>1.86 °C kg mol⁻¹</span>), ΔT_f = 1 × 1.86 × 1 = <span style='color: var(--warning);'>1.86 °C</span> depression.</p>
    
    <h4>4.3 Osmotic Pressure</h4>
    <p>Formula (ideal dilute solution): <strong>π = i·M·R·T</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>R</strong> = 0.0821 L atm K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ (or 8.314 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹).</li>
        <li><strong>T</strong> = temperature in Kelvin.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Mnemonic:</strong> “<em>Pressure = i × M × R × T</em> – think of “I’m Raining Today”.</p>
    
    <h4>4.4 Vapor‑Pressure Lowering (Raoult’s Law)</h4>
    <p>For a non‑volatile solute: <strong>P_solution = X_solvent × P⁰_solvent</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>P⁰_solvent</strong> – Vapor pressure of pure solvent.</li>
        <li><strong>X_solvent</strong> – Mole fraction of solvent.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>ΔP = P⁰_solvent – P_solution = X_solute × P⁰_solvent (since X_solvent + X_solute = 1).</p>
    
    <h3>5. Comparative Table – Colligative Properties</h3>
    <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; margin-bottom:15px;">
        <tr style="background:#e8f4ff;">
            <th>Property</th>
            <th>Formula</th>
            <th>Dependent on</th>
            <th>Typical Constant (Water)</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Boiling‑point elevation</strong></td>
            <td>ΔT_b = i·K_b·m</td>
            <td>Number of solute particles (i) & molality (m)</td>
            <td>K_b = <span style='color: var(--warning);'>0.512 °C kg mol⁻¹</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Freezing‑point depression</strong></td>
            <td>ΔT_f = i·K_f·m</td>
            <td>i & m</td>
            <td>K_f = <span style='color: var(--warning);'>1.86 °C kg mol⁻¹</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Osmotic pressure</strong></td>
            <td>π = i·M·R·T</td>
            <td>i, molarity (M) & absolute temperature (T)</td>
            <td>R = 0.0821 L atm K⁻¹ mol⁻¹</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Vapor‑pressure lowering</strong></td>
            <td>P = X_solvent·P⁰</td>
            <td>Mol fraction of solvent (X_solvent)</td>
            <td>Depends on solvent (e.g., water P⁰ at 25 °C = <span style='color: var(--warning);'>23.8 mmHg</span>)</td>
        </tr>
    </table>
    
    <h3>6. Van’t Hoff Factor (i) – Quick Guide</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>i = 1 for non‑electrolytes (e.g., glucose, sucrose).</li>
        <li>i ≈ number of ions formed for electrolytes:
            <ul>
                <li>NaCl → i ≈ 2</li>
                <li>CaCl₂ → i ≈ 3</li>
                <li>MgSO₄ → i ≈ 2</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li>Real solutions may show <strong>i < theoretical</strong> due to ion pairing; use experimental data for high‑precision questions.</li>
    </ul>
    
    <h3>7. Common Exam Tips & Tricks</h3>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Unit Consistency</strong> – Always convert concentrations to the unit required by the formula (molality for ΔT_b/ΔT_f, molarity for π).</li>
        <li><strong>“BFPV” Shortcut</strong> – When a question asks “Which property will change?”, instantly think of the four colligative effects.</li>
        <li><strong>i‑value Guess</strong> – For salts, assume complete dissociation unless the question mentions “weak electrolyte” or gives conductivity data.</li>
        <li><strong>Temperature Conversion</strong> – Remember <span style='color: var(--warning);'>T(K) = T(°C) + 273</span>. A quick mental addition of 273 saves time.</li>
        <li><strong>Raoult’s Law vs. Henry’s Law</strong> – Raoult’s applies to **solvent** vapor pressure; Henry’s is for **solute** gases over liquids. Keep the distinction clear.</li>
        <li><strong>Supersaturation</strong> – If a solution is described as “supersaturated”, it is **unstable** and can crystallize on disturbance – a frequent conceptual MCQ.</li>
        <li><strong>Check the Solvent</strong> – Constants K_b and K_f are solvent‑specific. Never use water values for ethanol or benzene questions.</li>
    </ol>
    
    <h3>8. Quick Reference – Constants for Common Solvents</h3>
    <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; margin-bottom:15px;">
        <tr style="background:#f9f9f9;">
            <th>Solvent</th>
            <th>K_b (°C kg mol⁻¹)</th>
            <th>K_f (°C kg mol⁻¹)</th>
            <th>P⁰ at 25 °C (mmHg)</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Water</td>
            <td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>0.512</span></td>
            <td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>1.86</span></td>
            <td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>23.8</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Benzene</td>
            <td>2.53</td>
            <td>5.12</td>
            <td>95.1</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Ethanol</td>
            <td>1.07</td>
            <td>1.99</td>
            <td>44.6</td>
        </tr>
    </table>
    
    <h3>9. Sample Numerical (NDA/CDS Style)</h3>
    <p><strong>Q:</strong> A 0.200 m solution of CaCl₂ is prepared at 298 K. Calculate the boiling‑point elevation of the solution. (K_b for water = 0.512 °C kg mol⁻¹)</p>
    <p><strong>Solution:</strong></p>
    <ol>
        <li>Determine i for CaCl₂ → i = 3 (Ca²⁺ + 2Cl⁻).</li>
        <li>Use ΔT_b = i·K_b·m = 3 × 0.512 × 0.200 = <span style='color: var(--warning);'>0.307 °C</span>.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Answer: <strong>0.31 °C (approx.)</strong></p>
    
    <h3>10. Summary – What Must You Remember?</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Definitions of solution types and the meaning of “homogeneous”.</li>
        <li>All concentration units with their symbols and conversion relations.</li>
        <li>“BFPV” for colligative properties and the corresponding formulas.</li>
        <li>Van’t Hoff factor (i) and its impact on ΔT_b, ΔT_f, and π.</li>
        <li>Key constants K_b, K_f, and vapor pressures for water and other common solvents.</li>
        <li>Exam‑friendly mnemonics: “MOLAR BOP”, “BFPV”, “I’m Raining Today”.</li>
        <li>Quick tip: For dilute aqueous solutions, treat M ≈ m to save time.</li>
    </ul>
    
    <p style="text-align:center; font-weight:bold; margin-top:20px;">Happy Revision – Crack Those Exams!</p>
</div>
`;

