window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["ai-gen-chemistry-chemical-bonding-ionic-covalent-coordinate-metallic-bonds"] = `
<div class="revision-card">
    <h1>Chemical Bonding – Ionic, Covalent, Coordinate & Metallic Bonds</h1>
    
    <h2>1. Quick Overview</h2>
    <p>Bonding explains how atoms achieve <strong>stable electronic configurations</strong>. The four major types examined for NDA/CDS/AFCAT are:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Ionic Bond</strong> – electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions.</li>
        <li><strong>Covalent Bond</strong> – sharing of electron pairs between non‑metals.</li>
        <li><strong>Coordinate (Dative) Bond</strong> – both electrons in the shared pair come from the same atom.</li>
        <li><strong>Metallic Bond</strong> – delocalised “sea of electrons” binding metal cations.</li>
    </ul>
    
    <h2>2. Ionic Bonding</h2>
    <h3>2.1 Definition & Formation</h3>
    <p>An ionic bond forms when the <strong>electronegativity difference (Δχ)</strong> between two atoms is ≥ <span style='color: var(--warning);'>1.7</span>. The more electronegative atom gains electrons becoming an anion (X⁻), while the less electronegative atom loses electrons becoming a cation (Y⁺).</p>
    
    <h3>2.2 Key Features</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>High <strong>lattice energy</strong> – energy released when gaseous ions combine to form a solid lattice.</li>
        <li>Typically form crystalline solids with high melting & boiling points.</li>
        <li>Conduct electricity in molten or aqueous state only.</li>
        <li>Soluble in polar solvents (water) due to ion‑dipole interactions.</li>
    </ul>
    
    <h3>2.3 Important Formula – Lattice Energy (Born‑Landé Equation)</h3>
    <p>
        <strong>U = – N_A·M·z⁺·z⁻·e² / (4πϵ₀·r₀)·(1 – 1/n)</strong><br>
        where<br>
        <strong>N_A</strong> = Avogadro’s number,<br>
        <strong>M</strong> = Madelung constant (depends on crystal geometry),<br>
        <strong>z⁺, z⁻</strong> = charges on cation & anion,<br>
        <strong>e</strong> = elementary charge,<br>
        <strong>r₀</strong> = distance between ion centres (≈ sum of ionic radii),<br>
        <strong>n</strong> = Born exponent (≈ <span style='color: var(--warning);'>9–12</span> for most salts).<br>
        <em>Exam tip:</em> For comparative questions, remember that lattice energy ↑ with higher charges and ↓ with larger ionic radii.
    </p>
    
    <h3>2.4 Representative Examples</h3>
    <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0">
        <tr><th>Compound</th><th>Formula</th><th>Δχ (approx.)</th><th>Type of Ionic Bond</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Sodium chloride</td><td>NaCl</td><td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>2.23</span></td><td>Na⁺ – Cl⁻</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Magnesium oxide</td><td>MgO</td><td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>2.71</span></td><td>Mg²⁺ – O²⁻</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Potassium bromide</td><td>KBr</td><td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>2.10</span></td><td>K⁺ – Br⁻</td></tr>
    </table>
    
    <h3>2.5 Mnemonic for Δχ Classification</h3>
    <p><strong>“Ionic > 1.7, Polar 0.4‑1.7, Non‑Polar ≤ 0.4”</strong> → <em>I</em>onic, <em>P</em>olar, <em>N</em>on‑polar.</p>
    
    <h2>3. Covalent Bonding</h2>
    <h3>3.1 Definition & Types</h3>
    <p>Covalent bonds involve the <strong>sharing of one or more electron pairs</strong> between two non‑metals. Classification is based on the number of shared pairs:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Single bond (σ)</strong> – one shared pair.</li>
        <li><strong>Double bond (σ + π)</strong> – two shared pairs.</li>
        <li><strong>Triple bond (σ + 2π)</strong> – three shared pairs.</li>
    </ul>
    
    <h3>3.2 Electronegativity Difference (Δχ) & Bond Polarity</h3>
    <p>
        <strong>Δχ ≤ <span style='color: var(--warning);'>0.4</span></strong> → Non‑polar covalent (e.g., H₂, Cl₂).<br>
        <strong>0.4 < Δχ < <span style='color: var(--warning);'>1.7</span></strong> → Polar covalent (e.g., HCl, H₂O).<br>
        <strong>Δχ ≥ <span style='color: var(--warning);'>1.7</span></strong> → Ionic (treated separately).<br>
        <em>Exam tip:</em> Remember the “<strong>0.4‑1.7 window</strong>” for covalent bonds.
    </p>
    
    <h3>3.3 Bond Length & Bond Energy</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Bond length</strong> decreases as bond order increases (single > double > triple).</li>
        <li><strong>Bond energy</strong> (kJ mol⁻¹) increases with bond order. Typical values:
            <ul>
                <li>C–C single: <span style='color: var(--warning);'>347</span></li>
                <li>C=C double: <span style='color: var(--warning);'>614</span></li>
                <li>C≡C triple: <span style='color: var(--warning);'>839</span></li>
            </ul>
        </li>
    </ul>
    
    <h3>3.4 Hybridisation & Geometry</h3>
    <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0">
        <tr><th>Hybridisation</th><th>Geometry</th><th>Bond Angle</th><th>Example</th></tr>
        <tr><td>sp</td><td>Linear</td><td>180°</td><td>CO₂, BeCl₂</td></tr>
        <tr><td>sp²</td><td>Trigonal planar</td><td>120°</td><td>BF₃, Ethene (C₂H₄)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>sp³</td><td>Tetrahedral</td><td>109.5°</td><td>CH₄, NH₃ (with lone pair)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>sp³d</td><td>Trigonal bipyramidal</td><td>90°/120°</td><td>PCl₅</td></tr>
        <tr><td>sp³d²</td><td>Octahedral</td><td>90°</td><td>SF₆</td></tr>
    </table>
    
    <h3>3.5 Important Concepts for Exams</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>VSEPR Theory</strong> – predicts shape from electron‑pair repulsion.</li>
        <li><strong>Resonance</strong> – delocalisation of π‑electrons; draw all valid Lewis structures and use the <em>double‑slash</em> symbol (↔).</li>
        <li><strong>Bond polarity vs. molecular polarity</strong> – a molecule can be non‑polar despite polar bonds (e.g., CO₂).</li>
    </ul>
    
    <h2>4. Coordinate (Dative) Bonding</h2>
    <h3>4.1 Definition</h3>
    <p>A coordinate bond is a special covalent bond where **both electrons** in the shared pair originate from the same atom (the **donor**). The accepting atom (the **acceptor**) provides an empty orbital.</p>
    
    <h3>4.2 Common Donor‑Acceptor Pairs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Ammonia (NH₃) → <strong>NH₃ → H⁺</strong> forming <strong>NH₄⁺</strong>.</li>
        <li>Water (O: lone pair) → <strong>H⁺</strong> → <strong>H₃O⁺</strong>.</li>
        <li>Carbonyl oxygen in <strong>metal carbonyls</strong> (e.g., Fe(CO)₅).</li>
    </ul>
    
    <h3>4.3 Distinguishing Features</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Bond length is usually **slightly longer** than a normal covalent bond of the same order.</li>
        <li>Often shown with an arrow (→) in Lewis structures to indicate direction of electron donation.</li>
        <li>In coordination chemistry, the central metal often forms multiple coordinate bonds with ligands (e.g., [Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺).</li>
    </ul>
    
    <h3>4.4 Mnemonic</h3>
    <p><strong>“Donor Gives, Acceptor Takes – D‑G‑A‑T”</strong> – remember that the donor **gives** both electrons.</p>
    
    <h2>5. Metallic Bonding</h2>
    <h3>5.1 Definition</h3>
    <p>Metallic bonding is characterised by a **‘sea of delocalised electrons’** moving freely over a lattice of positive metal ions. This model explains the typical metallic properties.</p>
    
    <h3>5.2 Key Properties Explained</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Electrical conductivity</strong> – free electrons carry charge.</li>
        <li><strong>Thermal conductivity</strong> – electrons transport kinetic energy.</li>
        <li><strong>Malleability & ductility</strong> – layers of cations can slide without breaking the bond.</li>
        <li><strong>Luster</strong> – interaction of delocalised electrons with light.</li>
        <li>Generally high **melting & boiling points**, though transition metals may have lower values due to weaker metallic bonding.</li>
    </ul>
    
    <h3>5.3 Quantitative Aspect – Metallic Bond Energy</h3>
    <p>Approximate metallic bond energy can be estimated using the **cohesive energy** (energy required to separate one mole of solid into isolated atoms). Typical values:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Aluminium: <span style='color: var(--warning);'>324 kJ mol⁻¹</span></li>
        <li>Copper: <span style='color: var(--warning);'>337 kJ mol⁻¹</span></li>
        <li>Iron: <span style='color: var(--warning);'>415 kJ mol⁻¹</span></li>
    </ul>
    
    <h3>5.4 Comparison with Ionic & Covalent Bonds</h3>
    <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0">
        <tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Ionic</th><th>Covalent</th><th>Metallic</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Bonding Entity</td><td>Electrostatic attraction between ions</td><td>Shared electron pair(s)</td><td>Delocalised electron sea</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Typical Melting Point</td><td>High</td><td>Variable (low for molecular, high for network)</td><td>Generally high</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Electrical Conductivity</td><td>Only in molten/solution</td><td>Usually poor (except for conjugated systems)</td><td>Excellent (solid state)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Directionality</td><td>None (isotropic)</td><td>Highly directional</td><td>Non‑directional (isotropic)</td></tr>
    </table>
    
    <h2>6. Quick Comparison – At a Glance</h2>
    <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0">
        <tr><th>Bond Type</th><th>Formation Condition</th><th>Typical Δχ</th><th>Physical State</th><th>Conductivity (solid)</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Ionic</td><td>Transfer of electrons</td><td>≥ <span style='color: var(--warning);'>1.7</span></td><td>Crystalline solid</td><td>Non‑conducting (molten/solution conducts)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Covalent (non‑polar)</td><td>Equal sharing</td><td>≤ <span style='color: var(--warning);'>0.4</span></td><td>Molecular solids/gases</td><td>Insulator</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Covalent (polar)</td><td>Unequal sharing</td><td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>0.4‑1.7</span></td><td>Varies (liquids, gases)</td><td>Usually insulator</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Coordinate</td><td>Donor‑acceptor electron pair</td><td>Varies (depends on donor)</td><td>Often part of complex ions</td><td>Depends on overall lattice</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Metallic</td><td>Delocalised electrons</td><td>Not applicable</td><td>Metallic solid</td><td>Excellent conductor</td></tr>
    </table>
    
    <h2>7. Frequently Asked Exam Questions (with Tips)</h2>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Predict the type of bond in Na₂O.</strong> <em>Tip:</em> Δχ (Na‑O) ≈ <span style='color: var(--warning);'>2.23</span> → Ionic.</li>
        <li><strong>Calculate the lattice energy trend for LiF, NaCl, KBr.</strong> <em>Tip:</em> Lattice energy ↑ with higher charge & ↓ with larger ionic radii. Hence LiF > NaCl > KBr.</li>
        <li><strong>Identify the hybridisation in CO₂.</strong> <em>Tip:</strong> Linear geometry → sp hybridisation on C.</li>
        <li><strong>Write the Lewis structure of NH₄⁺ and indicate the coordinate bond.</strong> <em>Tip:</em> Use an arrow from N’s lone pair to H⁺.</li>
        <li><strong>Why do metals conduct electricity in solid state whereas ionic solids do not?</strong> <em>Tip:</em> Recall the presence of a mobile electron sea in metals vs. fixed ions in ionic lattices.</li>
    </ol>
    
    <h2>8. Mnemonics & Memory Aids</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>“Ionic > 1.7, Polar 0.4‑1.7, Non‑Polar ≤ 0.4”</strong> – quick Δχ classification.</li>
        <li><strong>“SP³ = 4‑pointed star”</strong> – tetrahedral geometry (4 bonds).</li>
        <li><strong>“D‑G‑A‑T” (Donor Gives, Acceptor Takes)</strong> – coordinate bond direction.</li>
        <li><strong>“MELT – Metallic, Electro‑conductive, Lattice‑soft, Tough”</strong> – properties of metallic bonding.</li>
    </ul>
    
    <h2>9. Final Exam Checklist</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Know the Δχ thresholds and be able to apply them quickly.</li>
        <li>Memorise typical bond energies and lattice energy trends.</li>
        <li>Be comfortable drawing Lewis structures with coordinate bonds and indicating resonance
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["ai-gen-chemistry-thermochemistry-enthalpy-hess-s-law-bond-energies"] = `
<div class="revision-card">
<h2>Thermochemistry – Enthalpy, Hess’s Law &amp; Bond Energies</h2>

<h3>1. Basic Concepts</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Thermochemistry</strong> is the branch of chemistry that studies <strong>heat changes</strong> during chemical reactions.</li>
<li><strong>First Law of Thermodynamics</strong>: Energy can neither be created nor destroyed — <strong>ΔU = q + w</strong> (q = heat, w = work).</li>
<li><strong>System</strong>: Part of universe under study. <strong>Surroundings</strong>: Everything else.</li>
<li><strong>Endothermic Reaction</strong>: Absorbs heat (ΔH &gt; 0). <strong>Exothermic Reaction</strong>: Releases heat (ΔH &lt; 0).</li>
<li><strong>Enthalpy (H)</strong>: Heat content at constant pressure. <strong>ΔH = H<sub>products</sub> – H<sub>reactants</sub></strong>.</li>
<li>Units: kJ/mol or kcal/mol. 1 kcal = <span style='color: var(--warning);'>4.184 kJ</span>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Enthalpy Change (ΔH)</h3>
<ul>
<li>At constant pressure: <strong>ΔH = q<sub>p</sub></strong> (heat absorbed/released).</li>
<li>Standard conditions: 1 atm pressure, 25°C (<span style='color: var(--warning);'>298 K</span>), solutions in 1 M concentration.</li>
<li><strong>Standard Enthalpy of Reaction (ΔH°<sub>r</sub>)</strong>: Enthalpy change under standard conditions.</li>
<li><strong>Standard Enthalpy of Formation (ΔH°<sub>f</sub>)</strong>: Enthalpy change when 1 mole of compound is formed from its elements in standard state.</li>
<li><strong>Important:</strong> ΔH°<sub>f</sub> of elements in standard state = <span style='color: var(--warning);'>0</span>.</li>
<li><strong>Standard Enthalpy of Combustion (ΔH°<sub>c</sub>)</strong>: Heat released when 1 mole of substance is completely burnt in oxygen.</li>
<li><strong>Standard Enthalpy of Neutralization (ΔH°<sub>neut</sub>)</strong>: Heat released when 1 mole of water is formed from acid and base. Typical value = <span style='color: var(--warning);'>-57.1 kJ/mol</span>.</li>
<li><strong>Standard Enthalpy of Atomization (ΔH°<sub>atm</sub>)</strong>: Enthalpy change when 1 mole of gaseous atoms is formed from element in standard state.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3. Hess’s Law</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Total Enthalpy Change</strong> for a reaction is <strong>independent of the pathway</strong> — depends only on initial and final states.</li>
<li>Mathematical form: <strong>ΔH<sub>total</sub> = ΔH<sub>1</sub> + ΔH<sub>2</sub> + …</strong></li>
<li><strong>Application:</strong> Used to calculate unknown ΔH values using known data.</li>
<li><strong>Experimental Verification:</strong> Used to verify standard enthalpy of formation.</li>
<li><strong>Exam Tip:</strong> If a reaction is reversed, change the sign of ΔH. If coefficients are multiplied, multiply ΔH accordingly.</li>
</ul>

<h4>Example:</h4>
<p>Given:</p>
<ul>
<li>C(s) + O<sub>2</sub>(g) → CO<sub>2</sub>(g); ΔH = <span style='color: var(--warning);'>-393.5 kJ/mol</span></li>
<li>2C(s) + O<sub>2</sub>(g) → 2CO(g); ΔH = <span style='color: var(--warning);'>-221.0 kJ/mol</span></li>
<p>Find ΔH for CO(g) + ½O<sub>2</sub>(g) → CO<sub>2</sub>(g).</p>
</ul>
<p>Solution: Reverse reaction 2 and halve it; subtract from reaction 1. ΔH = -393.5 - (-110.5) = <span style='color: var(--warning);'>-283.0 kJ/mol</span>.</p>

<h3>4. Bond Energies (Bond Enthalpies)</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Bond dissociation energy</strong>: Energy required to break 1 mole of bonds in gaseous molecules.</li>
<li><strong>Average bond energy</strong>: Average energy required to break a particular bond in different molecules.</li>
<li><strong>Formula:</strong> <strong>ΔH = ΣBond energies of bonds broken – ΣBond energies of bonds formed</strong></li>
<li><strong>Sign convention:</strong> Breaking bonds (endothermic, +), forming bonds (exothermic, -).</li>
<li><strong>Limitation:</strong> Gives approximate values due to averaging.</li>
<li><strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Always use bond energies of gaseous molecules; liquids/solids must be vaporized first.</li>
</ul>

<h4>Common Bond Energies (kJ/mol):</h4>
<table border="1" cellpadding="6" cellspacing="0">
<tr><th>Bond</th><th>Bond Energy (kJ/mol)</th></tr>
<tr><td>H-H</td><td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>436</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>C-H</td><td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>413</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>C-C</td><td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>348</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>C=C</td><td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>614</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>C-O</td><td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>358</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>O=O</td><td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>498</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>N≡N</td><td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>945</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>O-H</td><td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>463</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h3>5. Kirchhoff’s Equation</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>ΔH<sub>2</sub> – ΔH<sub>1</sub> = Cp( T<sub>2</sub> – T<sub>1</sub>)</strong></li>
<li>Relates change in enthalpy with temperature at constant pressure.</li>
<li>Useful for calculating ΔH at different temperatures.</li>
</ul>

<h3>6. Key Comparisons</h3>
<table border="1" cellpadding="6" cellspacing="0">
<tr><th>Concept</th><th>Definition</th><th>Sign</th></tr>
<tr><td>Endothermic</td><td>Heat absorbed</td><td>ΔH &gt; 0</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exothermic</td><td>Heat evolved</td><td>ΔH &lt; 0</td></tr>
<tr><td>Enthalpy of Formation</td><td>Formation from elements</td><td>Varied</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bond Energy</td><td>Energy to break bonds</td><td>Always positive</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>7. Mnemonics &amp; Exam Tips</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mnemonic:</strong> “<em>Fire Up Cool Down</em>” — <strong>Formation, Combustion, Neutralization</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>Tip:</strong> Standard enthalpy of formation of O<sub>2</sub>, N<sub>2</sub>, H<sub>2</sub>, C(graphite) = 0.</li>
<li><strong>Tip:</strong> For bond energy calculations, always subtract bond formation energy from bond breaking energy.</li>
<li><strong>Tip:</strong> Hess’s Law is used in Born-Haber cycle for lattice energy calculations.</li>
<li><strong>Tip:</strong> ΔH°<sub>neut</sub> ≈ -57.1 kJ/mol for strong acid + strong base reaction.</li>
</ul>

<h3>8. Important Reactions &amp; Standard ΔH° Values</h3>
<table border="1" cellpadding="6" cellspacing="0">
<tr><th>Reaction</th><th>ΔH° (kJ/mol)</th></tr>
<tr><td>H<sub>2</sub>(g) + ½O<sub>2</sub>(g) → H<sub>2</sub>O(l)</td><td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>-285.8</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>C(s) + O<sub>2</sub>(g) → CO<sub>2</sub>(g)</td><td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>-393.5</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>CH<sub>4</sub>(g) + 2O<sub>2</sub>(g) → CO<sub>2</sub>(g) + 2H<sub>2</sub>O(l)</td><td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>-890.4</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>N<sub>2</sub>(g) + 3H<sub>2</sub>(g) → 2NH<sub>3</sub>(g)</td><td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>-92.4</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h3>9. Summary of Formulas</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>ΔH<sub>r</sub>° = Σ ΔH°<sub>f(products)</sub> – Σ ΔH°<sub>f(reactants)</sub></strong></li>
<li><strong>ΔH = Σ(BE<sub>reactants</sub>) – Σ(BE<sub>products</sub>)</strong></li>
<li><strong>q = mcΔT</strong> (heat absorbed)</li>
<li><strong>Kirchhoff’s Law:</strong> ΔH<sub>2</sub> – ΔH<sub>1</sub> = Cp(T<sub>2</sub> – T<sub>1</sub>)</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Final Exam Tip:</strong> Revise standard enthalpy of formation values of common compounds like CO<sub>2</sub>, H<sub>2</sub>O, NH<sub>3</sub>, and memorize key bond energy values for quick estimation-based questions in NDA and CDS!</strong></p>
</div>
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["ai-gen-chemistry-chemical-equilibrium-le-chatelier-s-principle-kp-kc"] = `
<div class="revision-card">
    <h1>Physical Chemistry – Chemical Equilibrium</h1>

    <h2>1. Fundamentals of Chemical Equilibrium</h2>
    <p>
        A reversible reaction reaches a state where the <strong>rate of the forward reaction</strong> equals the <strong>rate of the reverse reaction</strong>. At this point the concentrations (or partial pressures) of all species remain constant with time – this is the <strong>dynamic equilibrium</strong>.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>Only the *rates* become equal; the reaction continues to occur in both directions.</li>
        <li>The equilibrium composition depends only on temperature, pressure (for gases), and the nature of the reactants/products.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>2. The Equilibrium Constant</h2>
    <h3>2.1. Definition (Kc)</h3>
    <p>
        For a general gas‑phase reaction:
    </p>
    <p style="text-align:center;">
        <strong>aA + bB ⇌ cC + dD</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
        The concentration equilibrium constant (<strong>Kc</strong>) is:
    </p>
    <p style="text-align:center;">
        <strong>Kc = \\(\\frac{[C]^c [D]^d}{[A]^a [B]^b}\\)</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>[X]</strong> denotes the molar concentration (mol L<sup>‑1</sup>) of species X at equilibrium.
    </p>

    <h3>2.2. Definition (Kp)</h3>
    <p>
        When dealing with gases, the partial‑pressure equilibrium constant (<strong>Kp</strong>) is used:
    </p>
    <p style="text-align:center;">
        <strong>Kp = \\(\\frac{(P_C)^c (P_D)^d}{(P_A)^a (P_B)^b}\\)</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>P_X</strong> is the equilibrium partial pressure of X (in atm or bar).
    </p>

    <h3>2.3. Relationship between Kp and Kc</h3>
    <p>
        The two constants are linked by the ideal‑gas relation:
    </p>
    <p style="text-align:center;">
        <strong>Kp = Kc (RT)^{\\Delta n}</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
        where <strong>Δn = (c + d) – (a + b)</strong> is the change in the number of moles of gas, <strong>R = 0.0821 L·atm·K<sup>‑1</sup>·mol<sup>‑1</sup></strong>, and <strong>T</strong> is the absolute temperature (K).<br>
        <em>Mnemonic:</em> “<strong>Kp = Kc (RT)Δn</strong> – *K*eep *p*ressure *c*onsistent with *Δn*.”
    </p>

    <h2>3. Le Chatelier’s Principle</h2>
    <p>
        When a system at equilibrium is subjected to a change in <strong>concentration, pressure, temperature, or a catalyst</strong>, it will shift to counteract that change and re‑establish equilibrium.
    </p>

    <h3>3.1. Effect of Concentration</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Adding a reactant or removing a product drives the reaction to the <strong>right (forward)</strong>.</li>
        <li>Adding a product or removing a reactant drives the reaction to the <strong>left (reverse)</strong>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3.2. Effect of Pressure (or Volume) – Gaseous Systems</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Increasing pressure (decreasing volume) favours the side with <strong>fewer moles of gas (Δn < 0)</strong>.</li>
        <li>Decreasing pressure favours the side with <strong>more moles of gas (Δn > 0)</strong>.</li>
        <li>If Δn = 0, pressure changes have <strong>no effect on Kp or composition</strong>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3.3. Effect of Temperature</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>For an <strong>exothermic</strong> reaction (ΔH < 0), heat is treated as a product.
            <ul>
                <li>Increasing temperature shifts equilibrium to the <strong>left</strong> (endothermic direction).</li>
                <li>Decreasing temperature shifts it to the <strong>right</strong>.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li>For an <strong>endothermic</strong> reaction (ΔH > 0), heat is treated as a reactant.
            <ul>
                <li>Increasing temperature shifts equilibrium to the <strong>right</strong>.</li>
                <li>Decreasing temperature shifts it to the <strong>left</strong>.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3.4. Effect of a Catalyst</h3>
    <p>
        A catalyst speeds up both forward and reverse reactions equally; <strong>it does not change the position of equilibrium or the value of K</strong>.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Quantitative Use of Equilibrium Constants</h2>
    <h3>4.1. Solving Equilibrium Problems (ICE Table)</h3>
    <p>
        <strong>I</strong>nitial, <strong>C</strong>hange, <strong>E</strong>quilibrium (ICE) tables are the standard tool.
    </p>
    <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="margin:auto; width:90%;">
        <tr>
            <th>Species</th>
            <th>Initial (M or atm)</th>
            <th>Change (M or atm)</th>
            <th>Equilibrium (M or atm)</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>A</strong></td>
            <td>0.10</td>
            <td>–x</td>
            <td>0.10 – x</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>B</strong></td>
            <td>0.20</td>
            <td>–x</td>
            <td>0.20 – x</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>C</strong></td>
            <td>0</td>
            <td>+x</td>
            <td>x</td>
        </tr>
    </table>
    <p>
        Substitute the equilibrium expressions into the appropriate Kc or Kp equation and solve for <strong>x</strong>. Use approximations only when justified (e.g., x ≪ initial concentration).
    </p>

    <h3>4.2. Units of Kc and Kp</h3>
    <p>
        The equilibrium constant is *dimensionless* when expressed in terms of activities. In exam practice, however, you may be asked to write the *units*:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>For a reaction where Δn = 0, <strong>Kc</strong> and <strong>Kp</strong> are unit‑less.</li>
        <li>General rule: <strong>Kc</strong> units = (mol L<sup>‑1</sup>)<sup>Δn</sup>, <strong>Kp</strong> units = (atm)<sup>Δn</sup>.</li>
        <li>Example: For 2NO₂ ⇌ N₂O₄ (Δn = –1), Kc has units of <span style='color: var(--warning);'>L mol⁻¹</span> and Kp has units of <span style='color: var(--warning);'>atm⁻¹</span>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4.3. Temperature Dependence – Van’t Hoff Equation</h3>
    <p style="text-align:center;">
        <strong>ln K₂ – ln K₁ = –\\(\\frac{ΔH^\\circ}{R}\\) (1/T₂ – 1/T₁)</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
        Use this relation to predict how K changes with temperature when ΔH° is known.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Common Equilibrium Systems (Exam‑Ready)</h2>
    <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="margin:auto; width:100%;">
        <tr>
            <th>System</th>
            <th>Balanced Equation</th>
            <th>Δn (gas)</th>
            <th>Kc Expression</th>
            <th>Kp Expression</th>
            <th>ΔH° (kJ mol⁻¹)</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI</strong></td>
            <td>H₂(g) + I₂(g) ⇌ 2HI(g)</td>
            <td>0</td>
            <td>\\(\\frac{[HI]^2}{[H₂][I₂]}\\)</td>
            <td>\\(\\frac{(P_{HI})^2}{P_{H₂}P_{I₂}}\\)</td>
            <td>+180</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>N₂O₄ ⇌ 2NO₂</strong></td>
            <td>N₂O₄(g) ⇌ 2NO₂(g)</td>
            <td>+1</td>
            <td>\\(\\frac{[NO₂]^2}{[N₂O₄]}\\)</td>
            <td>\\(\\frac{(P_{NO₂})^2}{P_{N₂O₄}}\\)</td>
            <td>+57</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>NH₃ ⇌ ½N₂ + ½H₂</strong></td>
            <td>2NH₃(g) ⇌ N₂(g) + 3H₂(g)</td>
            <td>+2</td>
            <td>\\(\\frac{[N₂][H₂]^3}{[NH₃]^2}\\)</td>
            <td>\\(\\frac{(P_{N₂})(P_{H₂})^3}{(P_{NH₃})^2}\\)</td>
            <td>+92</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h2>6. Quick Revision Mnemonics</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>LE CHATELIER</strong> – <em>L</em>e <em>E</em>ffect of <em>C</em>oncentration, <em>H</em>igh <em>A</em>lteration of <em>T</em>emp, <em>E</em>ndothermic/Exothermic, <em>L</em>ow pressure, <em>I</em>nert catalyst, <em>E</em>quilibrium shift.</li>
        <li><strong>Kp‑Kc</strong> – “<span style='color: var(--warning);'>K</span>eep <span style='color: var(--warning);'>p</span>ressure <span style='color: var(--warning);'>c</span>onsistent – multiply by (RT)<sup>Δn</sup>”.</li>
        <li>For ΔH sign: “<strong>Exothermic = Heat out (product), Endothermic = Heat in (reactant)</strong>”.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>7. Exam Tips & Tricks</h2>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Read the question carefully</strong> – identify whether Kc or Kp is required; convert if necessary using the (RT)<sup>Δn</sup> relation.</li>
        <li>Always write the **balanced equation** first; an unbalanced equation gives wrong Δn and wrong K expression.</li>
        <li>When using ICE tables, <strong>check the magnitude of x</strong>. If x < 5 % of the initial concentration, the approximation “initial ≈ equilibrium” is acceptable.</li>
        <li>For temperature‑dependence problems, remember:
            <ul>
                <li>ΔH > 0 → K increases with T.</li>
                <li>ΔH < 0 → K decreases with T.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li>Units matter! If the question asks for the *numerical value* of K, give it **without units** (dimensionless). If units are requested, apply the Δn rule.</li>
        <li>Use the **Van’t Hoff plot** (ln K vs 1/T) only when multiple temperature data are given – the slope gives –ΔH/R.</li>
        <li>Remember that **catalysts do not affect K** – a common trap in MCQs.</li>
        <li>For gas‑phase equilibria, converting between concentration and pressure is often needed:
            <br><strong>[X] = \\(\\frac{P_X}{RT}\\)</strong>. Keep R consistent (0.0821 L·atm·K⁻¹·mol⁻¹).</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>8. Summary – Key Points to Memorise</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Equilibrium constant expression</strong> follows the law of mass action – products over reactants, each raised to its stoichiometric coefficient.</li>
        <li><strong>Kp = Kc (RT)^{Δn}</strong> – always check Δn.</li>
        <li>Le Chatelier’s principle predicts the *direction* of shift, not the magnitude.</li>
        <li>Temperature effect is governed by the sign of ΔH (exothermic vs endothermic).</li>
        <li>Pressure (or volume) changes affect only reactions where Δn ≠ 0.</li>
        <li>Catalysts accelerate the attainment of equilibrium but do **not** change K.</li>
        <li>Use ICE tables for quantitative problems; verify the validity of approximations.</li>
    </ul>

    <p style="text-align:center; font-weight:bold; margin-top:20px;">
        Good luck with your NDA/CDS/AFCAT preparation – Master the equilibrium concepts and you’ll ace the chemistry section!
    </p>
</div>
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["ai-gen-chemistry-electrochemistry-electrolysis-electrochemical-cells-corrosion"] = `
<div class="revision-card">
  <h1>Electrochemistry – Electrolysis, Electrochemical Cells & Corrosion</h1>

  <h2>1. Fundamentals</h2>
  <p><strong>Electrochemistry</strong> deals with the inter‑conversion of chemical energy and electrical energy. It is the backbone of many defence‑related technologies such as batteries, metal plating, and corrosion control of weapons and aircraft.</p>

  <h3>Key Terminology</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Electrode</strong> – conductor through which electrons enter or leave the electrolyte.</li>
    <li><strong>Electrolyte</strong> – medium containing free ions that conduct electricity.</li>
    <li><strong>Oxidation</strong> – loss of electrons (occurs at the <strong>anode</strong>).</li>
    <li><strong>Reduction</strong> – gain of electrons (occurs at the <strong>cathode</strong>).</li>
    <li><strong>Cell EMF (E°cell)</strong> – the driving voltage of a cell under standard conditions.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>2. Electrolysis</h2>
  <p>Electrolysis is the forced, non‑spontaneous passage of electric current through an electrolyte to bring about a chemical change.</p>

  <h3>2.1 Types of Electrolytic Cells</h3>
  <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0">
    <tr>
      <th>Medium</th>
      <th>Typical Example</th>
      <th>Products at Electrodes</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Molten Salt</td>
      <td>Molten NaCl</td>
      <td>Anode: Cl₂(g) Cathode: Na(s)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Aqueous Solution</td>
      <td>CuSO₄ solution</td>
      <td>Anode: O₂(g) (or Cu²⁺ → Cu) Cathode: Cu(s)</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h3>2.2 Faraday’s Laws of Electrolysis</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>First Law</strong>: The mass (m) of a substance deposited or liberated at an electrode is directly proportional to the quantity of electricity (Q) passed.
      <br><strong>Formula:</strong> <em>m = Z · Q</em> where <em>Z</em> = electrochemical equivalent (g C⁻¹).</li>
    <li><strong>Second Law</strong>: For the same quantity of electricity, the masses of different substances deposited are proportional to their equivalent weights (E = M/n).
      <br><strong>Combined Form:</strong> <em>Q = I t = (n F) · (N)</em> where <em>F = 96 485 C mol⁻¹</em> (Faraday constant).</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>2.3 Calculation Template (Exam Tip)</h3>
  <p>When a question asks for mass of product, follow the steps:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Write the half‑reaction and identify <strong>n</strong> (electrons transferred).</li>
    <li>Calculate total charge: <em>Q = I t</em> (use <span style='color: var(--warning);'>I</span> in A, <span style='color: var(--warning);'>t</span> in s).</li>
    <li>Find moles of electrons: <em>nₑ = Q/F</em>.</li>
    <li>Convert to moles of product: <em>nₚ = nₑ / n</em>.</li>
    <li>Mass = nₚ · M (molar mass).</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>2.4 Mnemonic for Electrode Processes</h3>
  <p><strong>“Cations go to CAT (cathode) – ANODE is where Anions leave.”</strong> This helps avoid sign‑confusion in both galvanic and electrolytic cells.</p>

  <h2>3. Electrochemical Cells</h2>

  <h3>3.1 Galvanic (Voltaic) vs. Electrolytic Cells</h3>
  <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0">
    <tr>
      <th>Feature</th>
      <th>Galvanic Cell</th>
      <th>Electrolytic Cell</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Spontaneity</td>
      <td>Spontaneous (ΔG < 0)</td>
      <td>Non‑spontaneous (ΔG > 0)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>External Power</td>
      <td>Not required</td>
      <td>Required</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Electrode Polarity</td>
      <td>Anode (+), Cathode (–)</td>
      <td>Anode (–), Cathode (+)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Mnemonic</td>
      <td><strong>LEO the lion says GER</strong> – <em>L</em>oses <em>e</em>lectrons <em>O</em>xidation (anode), <em>G</em>ains <em>e</em>lectrons <em>R</em>eduction (cathode)</td>
      <td>Reverse of LEO‑GER</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h3>3.2 Cell Notation</h3>
  <p>General format: <em>Zn(s) | Zn²⁺(aq) || Cu²⁺(aq) | Cu(s)</em></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Left side = oxidation half‑cell (anode).</li>
    <li>Right side = reduction half‑cell (cathode).</li>
    <li>Double vertical line “||” denotes the salt bridge.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3.3 Standard Electrode Potentials (E°)</h3>
  <p>All values are at <span style='color: var(--warning);'>25 °C</span>, 1 M, 1 atm.</p>
  <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0">
    <tr><th>Half‑Reaction</th><th>E° (V)</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu(s)</td><td>+0.34</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Zn(s)</td><td>–0.76</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag(s)</td><td>+0.80</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Fe³⁺ + e⁻ → Fe²⁺</td><td>+0.77</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂(g)</td><td>0.00 (Standard Hydrogen Electrode)</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h3>3.4 Calculating Cell EMF</h3>
  <p><strong>Formula:</strong> <em>E°cell = E°cathode – E°anode</em></p>
  <p><strong>Example – Daniell Cell:</strong> Zn|Zn²⁺ || Cu²⁺|Cu</p>
  <ul>
    <li>E°cathode (Cu²⁺/Cu) = +0.34 V</li>
    <li>E°anode (Zn²⁺/Zn) = –0.76 V</li>
    <li><strong>E°cell = 0.34 – (–0.76) = +1.10 V</strong></li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3.5 Nernst Equation (Non‑standard Conditions)</h3>
  <p><strong>General Form:</strong></p>
  <p style="margin-left:20px;"><em>E = E° – (0.0592 V / n) · log Q</em>   (at <span style='color: var(--warning);'>25 °C</span>)</p>
  <p>Where <em>Q</em> = reaction quotient, <em>n</em> = electrons transferred.</p>
  <p><strong>Exam Tip:</strong> For temperature other than 25 °C, replace 0.0592 V with (RT / nF) · ln10.</p>

  <h3>3.6 Gibbs Free Energy Relation</h3>
  <p><strong>ΔG = –n F E°cell</strong></p>
  <p>Thus a positive EMF implies a negative ΔG (spontaneous).</p>

  <h2>4. Corrosion</h2>
  <p>Corrosion is the undesirable electrochemical oxidation of metals in their environment, leading to material loss and structural failure.</p>

  <h3>4.1 General Corrosion Reaction (Iron Example)</h3>
  <p>In presence of water and oxygen:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Oxidation (Anodic): <em>Fe → Fe²⁺ + 2e⁻</em></li>
    <li>Reduction (Cathodic): <em>O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻ → 4OH⁻</em></li>
    <li>Overall: <em>2Fe + O₂ + 2H₂O → 2Fe(OH)₂</em> → Fe₂O₃·nH₂O (rust)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>4.2 Factors Influencing Corrosion</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Electrolyte Conductivity</strong> – Saline or acidic media accelerate corrosion.</li>
    <li><strong>Oxygen Availability</strong> – Aerated solutions increase cathodic rate.</li>
    <li><strong>Temperature</strong> – Higher temperature raises reaction kinetics (Arrhenius).</li>
    <li><strong>Metal Potential</strong> – Metals with lower E° are more anodic.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>4.3 Types of Corrosion</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Uniform Corrosion</strong> – Even material loss; easiest to predict.</li>
    <li><strong>Pitting Corrosion</strong> – Localised attack; dangerous for aircraft alloys.</li>
    <li><strong>Galvanic Corrosion</strong> – Occurs when two dissimilar metals are electrically coupled.</li>
    <li><strong>Intergranular Corrosion</strong> – Attacks grain boundaries, common in heat‑treated steels.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>4.4 Prevention & Control Methods</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Cathodic Protection</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>Impressed current method – external DC source makes metal the cathode.</li>
        <li>Sacrificial anode method – attach a more active metal (Zn, Mg, Al) that corrodes preferentially.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Protective Coatings</strong> – Paints, galvanisation, anodising.</li>
    <li><strong>Alloying</strong> – Adding Cr, Ni, Mo to steel forms passive films.</li>
    <li><strong>Corrosion Inhibitors</strong> – e.g., phosphates, nitrites in cooling water.</li>
    <li><strong>Design Modifications</strong> – Avoid crevices, ensure proper drainage, use insulating joints.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>4.5 Pourbaix (E‑pH) Diagrams – Quick Reference</h3>
  <p>These diagrams map the thermodynamic stability of a metal as a function of potential (E) and pH.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Region below the <strong>hydrogen line (E = –0.059 pH)</strong> → metal is protected (cathodic).</li>
    <li>Region above the <strong>oxygen line (E = 1.23 – 0.059 pH)</strong> → oxidation dominates.</li>
    <li>For Fe, the stable domain of Fe²⁺ lies between these lines; outside, FeOₓ or Fe(OH)₃ forms.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>4.6 Corrosion Rate Calculation (Exam Tip)</h3>
  <p>Use the formula:</p>
  <p style="margin-left:20px;"><em>CR (mm yr⁻¹) = (K · I · t) / (n · A · ρ)</em></p>
  <p>where:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>K</strong> = constant (8.76 × 10⁴ for mm yr⁻¹ when I in A, t in h, A in cm², ρ in g cm⁻³).</li>
    <li><strong>I</strong> = corrosion current (A).</li>
    <li><strong>t</strong> = time (h).</li>
    <li><strong>n</strong> = electrons per metal atom.</li>
    <li><strong>A</strong> = exposed area (cm²).</li>
    <li><strong>ρ</strong> = density of metal (g cm⁻³).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>5. Quick Revision Checklist (For NDA/CDS/AFCAT)</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Memorise the <strong>standard electrode potentials</strong> table up to ±0.10 V accuracy.</li>
    <li>Remember the sign convention: <strong>anode (+) in galvanic, (–) in electrolytic.</strong></li>
    <li>Apply <strong>Faraday’s laws</strong> – always write the half‑reaction first to get <em>n</em>.</li>
    <li>Use the <strong>Nernst equation</strong> for any non‑standard EMF question; plug <span style='color: var(--warning);'>25 °C</span> constant 0.0592 V unless otherwise given.</li>
    <li>For corrosion, identify whether the problem is <strong>uniform, pitting or galvanic</strong> and pick the appropriate prevention method.</li>
    <li>Mnemonic recap: <strong>“Cations go to CAT – ANODE is where Anions leave; LEO‑GER for galvanic, reverse for electrolytic.”</strong></li>
    <li>Practice cell notation – write left‑hand side as oxidation, right‑hand side as reduction.</li>
    <li>Remember the relation <strong>ΔG = –n F E°</strong> – useful for quick spontaneity checks.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>6. Important Dates & Numbers (Highlighted)</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Faraday constant <span style='color: var(--warning);'>96 485 C mol⁻¹</span> (discovered <span style='color: var(--warning);'>1834</span>).</li>
    <li>Standard temperature for most data: <span style='color: var(--warning);'>25 °C</span> (298 K).</li>
    <li>Standard pressure for gases: <span style='color: var(--warning);'>1 atm</span>.</li>
    <li>First practical galvanic cell – <strong>Voltaic Pile</strong> by Alessandro Volta, <span style='color: var(--warning);'>1800</span>.</li>
    <li>Discovery of the N
`;

