window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["ai-gen-chemistry-organic-reactions-combustion-addition-substitution-polymerisation"] = `
<div class="revision-card">
<h2>Organic Reactions – Combustion, Addition, Substitution, Polymerisation</h2>

<h3>1. Combustion Reaction</h3>
<p><strong>Definition:</strong> A reaction in which an organic compound is completely burned in the presence of oxygen, producing carbon dioxide and water as the major products.</p>
<p><strong>General Formula:</strong></p>
<p style="text-align:center;">C<sub>x</sub>H<sub>y</sub> + O<sub>2</sub> → CO<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O</p>
<p><strong>Key Points:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Used to determine the empirical or molecular formula based on mass of CO<sub>2</sub> and H<sub>2</sub>O produced.</li>
<li><strong>Mnemonic for balancing:</strong> “<em>Fire eats fuel fully</em>” – Balance C → CO<sub>2</sub>, H → H<sub>2</sub>O, then O<sub>2</sub>.</li>
<li>Calorific value measured using a bomb calorimeter.</li>
<li>All hydrocarbons combust to CO<sub>2</sub> and H<sub>2</sub>O, but incomplete combustion yields CO or soot (C).</li>
</ul>

<h3>2. Addition Reactions</h3>
<p><strong>Definition:</strong> A reaction where molecules add across a double or triple bond without any by-product formation.</p>
<p><strong>Key Features:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Typical for alkenes, alkynes, and carbonyl compounds.</li>
<li>Unsaturated compounds become saturated.</li>
<li>No loss of small molecules; only addition occurs.</li>
</ul>

<h4>Important Addition Reactions:</h4>
<table border="1" cellpadding="6" cellspacing="0">
<tr><th>Reaction Type</th><th>Reagent</th><th>Product</th><th>Notes / Mechanism</th></tr>
<tr><td>Hydrogenation</td><td>H₂ + Pt/Pd/Ni</td><td>Alkane</td><td>Exothermic; used in margarine production.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Halogenation</td><td>X₂ (Cl₂/Br₂)</td><td>Halide derivative</td><td>Orange → Colourless; electrophilic addition.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hydrohalogenation</td><td>HX (HCl/HBr)</td><td>Alkyl halide</td><td>Follows Markovnikov's rule.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hydration</td><td>H₂O + H₃O⁺ catalyst</td><td>Alcohol</td><td>Acid-catalyzed; oxymercuration or H⁺ pathway.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Oxymercuration</td><td>Hg(OAc)₂ + H₂O</td><td>Alcohol</td><td>Anti-Markovnikov due to carbocation stabilization.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bromination of Alkyne</td><td>Br₂ (cold, dil.)</td><td>Dibromoalkene → Tetrabromoalkane</td><td>Controlled addition in two steps.</td></tr>
</ul>

<p><strong>Exam Tip:</strong> In asymmetric addition, <strong>Markovnikov’s Rule</strong> states: <em>H adds to the carbon with more hydrogens, X adds to the carbon with fewer hydrogens.</em></p>
<p><strong>Crammed Sentence:</strong> <em>“Happy Henry Keeps Happy Henry Clean”</em> – Hydrogenation, Halogenation, Hydrohalogenation, Hydration, Oxymercuration, Bromination.</p>

<h3>3. Substitution Reactions</h3>
<p><strong>Definition:</strong> A reaction where one atom or group is replaced by another. Common in haloalkanes and benzene derivatives.</p>

<h4>Types:</h4>

<h4>a) Nucleophilic Substitution – <em>S<sub>N</sub>1 & S<sub>N</sub>2</em></h4>
<table border="1" cellpadding="6" cellspacing="0">
<tr><th>Feature</th><th>S<sub>N</sub>2</th><th>S<sub>N</sub>1</th></tr>
<tr><td>Mechanism</td><td>Concerted (one step)</td><td>Two steps (carbocation intermediate)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Steric effect</td><td>Hindered substrates disfavored</td><td>Tertiary preferred</td></tr>
<tr><td>Racemization</td><td>Inversion of configuration</td><td>Partial racemization</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rate Law</td><td>Rate = k[RX][Nu⁻]</td><td>Rate = k[RX]</td></tr>
<tr><td>Example</td><td>CH₃CH₂Br + OH⁻ → CH₃CH₂OH + Br⁻</td><td>(CH₃)₃CBr → (CH₃)₃C⁺ + Br⁻</td></tr>
</table>

<h4>b) Electrophilic Substitution of Benzene</h4>
<p><strong>Common Examples:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Nitration:</strong> HNO₃ + H₂SO₄ → NO₂⁺ (nitronium ion) attacks benzene ring.</li>
<li><strong>Halogenation:</strong> X₂ + FeX₃ catalyst → electrophilic X⁺ attacks ring.</li>
<li><strong>Sulfonation:</strong> H₂SO₄ (conc.) → SO₃H group adds to ring.</li>
<li><strong>Alkylation:</strong> AlCl₃ catalyst → R⁺ attacks ring (can cause polyalkylation).</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Activation vs Deactivation:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Activating Groups:</strong> –OH, –NH₂, –OCH₃ (donate electrons, increase ring reactivity).</li>
<li><strong>Deactivating Groups:</strong> –NO₂, –CN, –COOH (withdraw electrons, decrease reactivity).</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Mnemonic:</strong> "<strong>An Oxymoron Helps Acid Damage Benzene</strong>" – Activating groups: <em>-OH, -NH₂, -OCH₃</em>, Acidic groups like -COOH, and <em>-NO₂</em> is deactivating.</p>

<h3>4. Polymerisation Reactions</h3>
<p><strong>Definition:</strong> Process of forming macromolecules (polymers) from monomers.</p>
<p><strong>Two Main Types:</strong></p>

<h4>a) Addition (Chain-growth) Polymerisation</h4>
<p><strong>Conditions:</strong> Usually initiated by heat, light, or radical initiators like benzoyl peroxide.</p>
<p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>PVC:</strong> Polymerisation of vinyl chloride → Polyvinyl chloride.</li>
<li><strong>Polyethylene:</strong> Ethene → –(CH₂–CH₂)ₙ–</li>
<li><strong>Polystyrene:</strong> Styrene monomer → rigid plastic.</li>
<li><strong>Poly(methyl methacrylate)</strong> (PMMA):<strong> Perspex/Lucite</strong></li>
</ul>

<h4>b) Condensation (Step-growth) Polymerisation</h4>
<p><strong>Characteristics:</strong> Water or small molecule eliminated per linkage formed.</p>
<p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Nylon-6,6:</strong> Hexamethylenediamine + Adipic acid → amide linkage.</li>
<li><strong>PET (Polyester):</strong> Terephthalic acid + Ethylene glycol → ester linkage.</li>
<li><strong>Polycarbonate:</strong> Bisphenol-A + Phosgene → carbonate linkage.</li>
<li><strong>Protein synthesis:</strong> Amino acids → Peptide bonds + H₂O</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Important Polymers Table:</strong></p>
<table border="1" cellpadding="6" cellspacing="0">
<tr><th>Polymer</th><th>Monomer(s)</th><th>Type</th><th>Use</th></tr>
<tr><td>Polyethylene</td><td>Ethylene</td><td>Addition</td><td>Packaging bags</td></tr>
<tr><td> PVC</td><td>Vinyl chloride</td><td>Addition</td><td>Pipes, bottles</td></tr>
<tr><td> Polystyrene</td><td>Styrene</td><td>Addition</td><td>Packaging, cutlery</td></tr>
<tr><td> Polyethylene terephthalate (PET)</td><td>Terephthalic acid + Ethylene glycol</td><td>Condensation</td><td>Beverage bottles</td></tr>
<tr><td> Nylon</td><td>Diamine + Diacid</td><td>Condensation</td><td>Fabrics, ropes</td></tr>
<tr><td> Bakelite</td><td>Phenol + Formaldehyde</td><td>Condensation</td><td>Electrical insulators</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>✅ Summary &amp; Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Combustion:</strong> Always ends with CO₂ + H₂O; vital for elemental analysis.</li>
<li><strong>Addition:</strong> Double/triple bonds break → single bonds; follows Markovnikov rule.</li>
<li><strong>Substitution:</strong> One group replaces another; S<sub>N</sub>1/S<sub>N</sub>2 differ in mechanism & stereochemistry.</li>
<li><strong>Polymerisation:</strong> Addition = no byproduct; condensation = H₂O or other small molecule eliminated.</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Exam Strategy:</strong> Practice predicting product structures using mechanistic arrows and apply rules like Markovnikov, Saytzeff, and orientation in benzene rings. Always label stereochemistry correctly for S<sub>N</sub>2 and elimination reactions.</p>

<p><strong>Quick Recall Mnemonics:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><em>"Only Addition Occurs Across Double Bonds"</em> – reinforces addition concept.</li>
<li><em>"Substitution Switch: Leave Behind, Bring In"</em> – helps remember substitution pattern.</li>
<li><em>"Plastic Paradise Made By Linking Molecules"</em> – polymerisation reminder.</li>
</ul>

</div>
<style>
.revision-card {
  font-family: 'Segoe UI', sans-serif;
  background-color: #fefefe;
  padding: 20px;
  border-left: 4px solid #007acc;
  box-shadow: 0 0 10px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);
}
h2 { color: #007acc; }
h3 { color: #333; margin-top: 18px; }
table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 10px 0; }
th, td { text-align: left; }
</style>
`;

