window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["ai-gen-biology-photosynthesis-light-dark-reactions-factors-affecting-c3-c4-plants"] = `
<div class="revision-card">
  <h2>Photosynthesis – Complete Revision</h2>

  <h3>1. Overview & Overall Equation</h3>
  <p><strong>Photosynthesis</strong> is the process by which green plants, algae and cyanobacteria convert solar energy into chemical energy stored as carbohydrates.</p>
  <p><strong>Overall balanced equation</strong> (C₃ pathway):</p>
  <pre style="background:#f9f9f9;padding:10px;">
<span style='color: var(--warning);'>6 CO₂</span> + <span style='color: var(--warning);'>6 H₂O</span> + light energy → <span style='color: var(--warning);'>C₆H₁₂O₆</span> + <span style='color: var(--warning);'>6 O₂</span>
  </pre>
  <p>Key point: Light energy is first captured in the <strong>light‑dependent reactions</strong> to produce <span style='color: var(--warning);'>ATP</span> and <span style='color: var(--warning);'>NADPH</span>, which then drive the <strong>dark (Calvin) reactions</strong> for carbon fixation.</p>

  <h3>2. Light‑Dependent Reactions (Thylakoid Phase)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Location:</strong> Thylakoid membranes of chloroplasts.</li>
    <li><strong>Main steps:</strong>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Photon absorption</strong> by <strong>Photosystem II (PSII)</strong> – pigment‑protein complex.</li>
        <li><strong>Water splitting (photolysis)</strong> → O₂ + 2H⁺ + 2e⁻. (Discovered 1931 – Hill reaction).</li>
        <li><strong>Electron transport chain (ETC)</strong> – electrons move from PSII → plastoquinone (PQ) → cytochrome b₆f → plastocyanin (PC) → <strong>Photosystem I (PSI)</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>ATP synthesis</strong> via chemiosmosis (photophosphorylation) by ATP synthase as H⁺ flow back from lumen to stroma.</li>
        <li><strong>NADP⁺ reduction</strong> at PSI → NADPH.</li>
      </ol>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Products per 2 photons (per water molecule):</strong> 1 ATP, 1 NADPH, ½ O₂.</li>
    <li><strong>Overall yield (per 18 photons):</strong> <span style='color: var(--warning);'>12 ATP</span> + <span style='color: var(--warning);'>8 NADPH</span> + <span style='color: var(--warning);'>O₂</span>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. Dark (Calvin‑Benson) Reactions – C₃ Cycle</h3>
  <p><strong>Location:</strong> Stroma of chloroplasts.</p>
  <p><strong>Key phases (3‑step cycle)</strong> – each turn fixes one CO₂ molecule.</p>
  <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;margin-top:10px;">
    <tr style="background:#e0e0e0;">
      <th>Phase</th>
      <th>Enzyme (Key)</th>
      <th>Major Products</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Carbon Fixation</td>
      <td>Ribulose‑1,5‑bisphosphate carboxylase/oxygenase (<strong>Rubisco</strong>)</td>
      <td>3‑Phosphoglycerate (3‑PGA) – <span style='color: var(--warning);'>2 molecules</span></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Reduction</td>
      <td>Phosphoglycerate kinase & Glyceraldehyde‑3‑phosphate dehydrogenase</td>
      <td>Glyceraldehyde‑3‑phosphate (G3P) – <span style='color: var(--warning);'>1 usable G3P</span> (others regenerate RuBP)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Regeneration</td>
      <td>RuBP‑carboxylase/oxygenase (Rubisco) & other enzymes</td>
      <td>Regenerates <span style='color: var(--warning);'>5 RuBP</span> using <span style='color: var(--warning;'>3 ATP</span></td>
    </tr>
  </table>
  <p><strong>Net requirement for synthesis of 1 glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆):</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><span style='color: var(--warning);'>6 CO₂</span></li>
    <li><span style='color: var(--warning);'>12 ATP</span></li>
    <li><span style='color: var(--warning);'>8 NADPH</span></li>
  </ul>

  <h3>4. Factors Affecting the Rate of Photosynthesis</h3>
  <p>Mnemonic – <strong>“L‑C‑T‑W‑C‑N”</strong> (Light, CO₂, Temperature, Water, Chlorophyll, Nutrients)</p>
  <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;margin-top:10px;">
    <tr style="background:#e0e0e0;">
      <th>Factor</th>
      <th>Effect on Rate</th>
      <th>Exam Tip</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Light Intensity</td>
      <td>Directly proportional up to saturation point (~<span style='color: var(--warning);'>10 000 lux</span> for most C₃ leaves).</td>
      <td>Remember the “Light‑saturation curve” – steep rise then plateau.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>CO₂ Concentration</td>
      <td>Higher <span style='color: var(--warning);'>CO₂</span> → faster Rubisco activity; limiting factor in closed chambers.</td>
      <td>Typical experimental value: <span style='color: var(--warning);'>0.03% (ambient)</span> vs <span style='color: var(--warning);'>0.1% (enriched)</span>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Temperature</td>
      <td>Optimal range 25‑35 °C for C₃; >40 °C denatures Rubisco & increases photorespiration.</td>
      <td>Remember “Q₁₀ rule”: rate roughly doubles for each 10 °C rise up to optimum.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Water Availability</td>
      <td>Stomatal closure → reduced CO₂ intake; prolonged drought → leaf wilting → lower chlorophyll.</td>
      <td>Link with “Transpiration‑CO₂ trade‑off”.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Chlorophyll Content</td>
      <td>Deficiency (e.g., nitrogen deficiency) → pale leaves → lower light capture.</td>
      <td>Mnemonic: “N‑green = chlorophyll”.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Nutrients (N, P, K, Mg)</td>
      <td>Mg is central atom of chlorophyll; N is part of Rubisco; P needed for ATP.</td>
      <td>Exam often asks “Which nutrient directly affects chlorophyll?” → <strong>Mg</strong>.</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h3>5. C₃ vs C₄ Plants – Comparative Table</h3>
  <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;margin-top:10px;">
    <tr style="background:#e0e0e0;">
      <th>Feature</th>
      <th>C₃ (Calvin) Plants</th>
      <th>C₄ (Hatch‑Slack) Plants</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Primary Carbon Fixation Enzyme</td>
      <td>Rubisco (carboxylase)</td>
      <td>PEP‑Carboxylase (phosphoenolpyruvate)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>First Stable Product</td>
      <td>3‑Phosphoglycerate (3‑PGA)</td>
      <td>Oxaloacetate → malate (C₄ acid)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Leaf Anatomy</td>
      <td>Homogenous mesophyll</td>
      <td>Kranz anatomy – bundle‑sheath cells surrounding vascular bundles</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Photorespiration</td>
      <td>High (especially >30 °C)</td>
      <td>Very low – CO₂ concentrated in bundle sheath</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Optimal Climate</td>
      <td>Cool, moist (temperate)</td>
      <td>Hot, dry, high‑light (tropical & subtropics)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Water‑Use Efficiency (WUE)</td>
      <td>~3 g biomass per kg water</td>
      <td>~6 g biomass per kg water (≈2× higher)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Examples</td>
      <td>Wheat, rice, soybean, barley</td>
      <td>Maize, sugarcane, sorghum, millet</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Key Evolutionary Date</td>
      <td>Origin ~<span style='color: var(--warning);'>150 Mya</span> (Cretaceous)</td>
      <td>Emergence ~<span style='color: var(--warning);'>30‑35 Mya</span> (Miocene)</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h4>Mnemonic to Remember C₄ Pathway Steps</h4>
  <p><strong>“PEP‑Carboxylase Packs OXAcid, Malate Moves, Decarboxylates, Calvin Fixes”</strong></p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>P</strong>EP‑Carboxylase fixes CO₂ → <strong>O</strong>xaloacetate.</li>
    <li><strong>M</strong>alate (or Aspartate) transports to bundle‑sheath.</li>
    <li><strong>D</strong>ecarboxylation releases CO₂ for Rubisco.</li>
    <li><strong>C</strong>alvin cycle (C₃) proceeds.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>6. Important Historical Milestones (Dates Highlighted)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><span style='color: var(--warning);'>1931</span> – Robert Hill demonstrates O₂ evolution (Hill reaction).</li>
    <li><span style='color: var(--warning);'>1937</span> – Discovery of chlorophyll fluorescence.</li>
    <li><span style='color: var(--warning);'>1955</span> – Calvin, Benson & Bassham elucidate the Calvin cycle (C₃ pathway).</li>
    <li><span style='color: var(--warning);'>1966</span> – Hatch & Slack propose the C₄ pathway.</li>
    <li><span style='color: var(--warning);'>1970s</span> – Development of the “O₂ electrode” for measuring photosynthetic O₂ evolution.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>7. Frequently Asked Exam Questions & Quick Tips</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Write the overall equation of photosynthesis and name the organelle where it occurs.</strong><br>
      <em>Tip:</em> Memorise the equation in the exact order of reactants → products; underline “chloroplast”.</li>
    <li><strong>Differentiate between light‑dependent and light‑independent reactions.</strong><br>
      <em>Tip:</em> Use a two‑column table; bold headings “Location”, “Products”, “Key pigment”.</li>
    <li><strong>Explain why C₄ plants are more efficient in arid regions.</strong><br>
      <em>Tip:</em> Mention Kranz anatomy, PEP‑carboxylase affinity, reduced photorespiration, higher WUE.</li>
    <li><strong>State the effect of temperature on the rate of photosynthesis and give the optimum range for C₃ plants.</strong><br>
      <em>Tip:</em> Quote “25‑35 °C” and add the Q₁₀ rule as a quick recall.</li>
    <li><strong>List the three phases of the Calvin cycle and the ATP/NADPH consumption in each.</strong><br>
      <em>Tip:</em> Use the table above; remember “3‑PGA → G3P (reduction) uses 1 ATP + 1 NADPH per CO₂”.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>8. Quick Revision Summary (Bullet Points)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Light reactions</strong> – PSII → H₂O split → O₂ + e⁻; electron flow → PSI; ATP via chemiosmosis; NADPH formed.</li>
    <li><strong>Calvin cycle</strong> – CO₂ + RuBP → 3‑PGA → G3P → Regeneration of RuBP.</li>
    <li><strong>Key numbers:</strong> <span style='color: var(--warning);'>12 ATP</span> + <span style='color: var(--warning);'>8 NADPH</span> per glucose; <span style='color: var(--warning);'>6 CO₂</span> fixed.</li>
    <li><strong>Factors:</strong> Light, CO₂, Temperature, Water, Chlorophyll, Nutrients (L‑C‑T‑W‑C‑N).</li>
    <li><strong>C₃ vs C₄:</strong> Rubisco vs PEP‑carboxylase; mesophyll vs Kranz; high vs low photorespiration; temperate vs tropical.</li>
    <li><strong>Exam tip:</strong> Whenever a question asks “Why is photorespiration wasteful?”, answer with “Rubisco oxygenase activity → 2‑phosphoglycolate → CO₂ loss & ATP consumption”.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>9. Visual Aid (Suggested Diagram for Hand‑written Revision)</h3>
  <p>Draw a single chloroplast showing:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Thylakoid stacks (grana) – label PSII, PSI, ATP synthase.</li>
    <li>Stroma – indicate Calvin cycle enzymes.</li>
    <li>For C₄, add bundle‑sheath cells surrounding a vascular bundle and label PEP‑carboxylase in mesophyll.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Colour‑code: <span style='color: var(--warning);'>Red</span> for O₂, <span style='color: var(--warning);'>Blue</span> for H₂O, <span style='color: var(--warning);'>Green</span> for CO₂.</p>

  <h3>10. Final Exam‑Ready Checklist</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Memorise the overall equation and the exact ATP/NADPH count.</li>
    <li>Be able to sketch and label the light‑reaction complexes.</li>
    <li>Recall the three Calvin‑cycle phases with their enzymes.</li>
    <li>List the six factors (L‑C‑T‑W‑C‑N) with one line of effect each.</li>
    <li>Contrast C₃ & C₄ in a table – focus on anatomy, enzyme, climate, examples.</li>
    <li>Remember key dates (1931, 1955, 1966) for quick “history” questions.</li>
    <li>Practice mnemonics: “PEP‑Carboxylase Packs OXAcid, Malate Moves, Decarboxylates, Calvin Fixes”.</li>
  </ol>

  <p><strong>Good luck!</strong> Use these notes for rapid recall
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["ai-gen-biology-plant-respiration-transpiration-mechanisms"] = `
<div class="revision-card">
    <h1>Plant Respiration & Transpiration – Mechanisms</h1>

    <h2>1. Plant Respiration</h2>
    <p><strong>Definition:</strong> Conversion of stored organic compounds (mainly glucose) into usable energy (ATP) with the release of <strong>CO₂</strong> and <strong>H₂O</strong>. It occurs in all living parts of the plant – roots, stems, leaves, fruits & seeds.</p>

    <h3>1.1 Overall Chemical Equation</h3>
    <p style="font-size:1.1em;">
        <strong>C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6 O₂ → 6 CO₂ + 6 H₂O + <span style='color: var(--warning);'>38 ATP</span></strong>
    </p>
    <p>Note: The ATP yield varies (30‑38) depending on the shuttle system (malate‑aspartate vs glycerol‑phosphate).</p>

    <h3>1.2 Stages of Aerobic Respiration</h3>
    <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; margin-bottom:15px;">
        <tr style="background:#f2f2f2;">
            <th>Stage</th>
            <th>Location</th>
            <th>Main Events</th>
            <th>ATP Yield</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Glycolysis</strong></td>
            <td>Cytosol</td>
            <td>Glucose → 2 Pyruvate + 2 NADH + 2 ATP (substrate‑level)</td>
            <td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>2</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Link Reaction (Pyruvate Decarboxylation)</strong></td>
            <td>Mitochondrial matrix</td>
            <td>2 Pyruvate → 2 Acetyl‑CoA + 2 CO₂ + 2 NADH</td>
            <td>0 (produces NADH)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Krebs Cycle (Citric Acid Cycle)</strong></td>
            <td>Mitochondrial matrix</td>
            <td>Each Acetyl‑CoA yields 3 NADH, 1 FADH₂, 1 GTP (≈1 ATP)</td>
            <td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>2</span> (GTP) + NADH/FADH₂ later used in ETC</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Electron Transport Chain (ETC) & Oxidative Phosphorylation</strong></td>
            <td>Inner mitochondrial membrane</td>
            <td>Electrons from NADH/FADH₂ travel through complexes → O₂ reduced to H₂O; proton gradient drives ATP synthase</td>
            <td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>34‑34</span> ATP (≈ 3 ATP per NADH, 2 ATP per FADH₂)</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h3>1.3 Anaerobic Respiration (Fermentation)</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Alcoholic Fermentation</strong> (common in fruits & germinating seeds):<br>
            <code>Glucose → 2 Ethanol + 2 CO₂ + 2 ATP</code></li>
        <li><strong>Lactic Acid Fermentation</strong> (rare in plants):<br>
            <code>Glucose → 2 Lactic Acid + 2 ATP</code></li>
    </ul>
    <p>Only <span style='color: var(--warning);'>2 ATP</span> are produced – used when O₂ is limiting.</p>

    <h3>1.4 Factors Influencing Respiration Rate</h3>
    <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; margin-bottom:15px;">
        <tr style="background:#f9f9f9;">
            <th>Factor</th>
            <th>Effect</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Temperature</td>
            <td>Rate ↑ up to optimum (<span style='color: var(--warning);'>25‑30 °C</span>) then ↓ (enzyme denaturation)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>O₂ Availability</td>
            <td>Low O₂ → shift to fermentation (↓ ATP)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Substrate Concentration</td>
            <td>Higher glucose → ↑ rate (Michaelis‑Menten kinetics)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>pH</td>
            <td>Neutral pH (≈7) optimal; extreme pH inhibits enzymes</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h3>1.5 Mnemonic for Respiration Stages</h3>
    <p><strong>G</strong>et <strong>K</strong>nowledge <strong>E</strong>very <strong>T</strong>ime → <strong>G</strong>lycolysis, <strong>K</strong>rebs, <strong>E</strong>TC, <strong>T</strong>otal ATP.</p>

    <h3>1.6 Exam Tips</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Always write the <strong>overall equation</strong> first – it fetches marks for completeness.</li>
        <li>Remember the **site**: Cytosol (glycolysis) vs Mitochondrial matrix (Krebs) vs Inner membrane (ETC).</li>
        <li>When asked “compare respiration & photosynthesis”, use the table below.</li>
        <li>For numerical questions, keep the ATP yield per NADH (≈3) and per FADH₂ (≈2) handy.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>2. Plant Transpiration</h2>
    <p><strong>Definition:</strong> Loss of water vapor from plant aerial parts, primarily through stomata. It drives the upward movement of water (transpiration pull) and cools the plant.</p>

    <h3>2.1 Pathway of Water Movement</h3>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Absorption</strong> – Roots take up water from soil (osmosis).</li>
        <li><strong>Ascent of Sap</strong> – Via <strong>root pressure</strong> (daytime) and <strong>capillary action</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Transport through Xylem</strong> – Cohesion‑tension mechanism.</li>
        <li><strong>Diffusion</strong> – From xylem to mesophyll cells.</li>
        <li><strong>Evaporation</strong> – From cell walls into intercellular air spaces.</li>
        <li><strong>Transpiration</strong> – Escape of water vapor through stomata to the atmosphere.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>2.2 Mechanisms of Water Loss</h3>
    <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; margin-bottom:15px;">
        <tr style="background:#e8f4f8;">
            <th>Pathway</th>
            <th>Percentage of Total Transpiration</th>
            <th>Key Features</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Stomatal Transpiration</strong></td>
            <td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>95‑99 %</span></td>
            <td>Through stomatal pores; regulated by guard cells (turgor changes).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Cuticular Transpiration</strong></td>
            <td><span style='color: var(--warning);'>1‑5 %</span></td>
            <td>Through the waxy cuticle; inversely related to cuticle thickness.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Lenticular (Hydathode) Transpiration</strong></td>
            <td>Negligible (<1 %)</td>
            <td>Occurs at leaf margins via hydathodes; important in guttation.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Aerial (Leaf‑Surface) Transpiration</strong></td>
            <td>Very low</td>
            <td>Direct diffusion through epidermal cells; significant only in very thin leaves.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h3>2.3 Guard Cell Physiology (Stomatal Opening & Closing)</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Opening Stimuli:</strong> Light (blue), low CO₂, high internal K⁺, ABA ↓, high humidity.</li>
        <li><strong>Closing Stimuli:</strong> Dark, high CO₂, drought (ABA ↑), low humidity, high external K⁺.</li>
        <li>Mechanism: Light → activation of H⁺‑ATPase → K⁺ influx → water influx → guard cells swell → pore opens.</li>
        <li>ABA triggers Ca²⁺ channels → K⁺ efflux → water exits → guard cells shrink → pore closes.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2.4 Factors Affecting Transpiration Rate</h3>
    <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; margin-bottom:15px;">
        <tr style="background:#f0f8ff;">
            <th>Factor</th>
            <th>Effect on Rate</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Temperature</td>
            <td>↑ Temperature → ↑ kinetic energy → ↑ evaporation (optimal <span style='color: var(--warning);'>25‑35 °C</span>).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Relative Humidity</td>
            <td>Low humidity → larger vapor pressure deficit → ↑ transpiration.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Wind Speed</td>
            <td>Higher wind → removes saturated boundary layer → ↑ transpiration.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Light Intensity</td>
            <td>Blue light activates guard‑cell H⁺‑ATPase → stomatal opening → ↑ transpiration.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Soil Water Availability</td>
            <td>Drought → ABA ↑ → stomatal closure → ↓ transpiration.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Leaf Anatomy</td>
            <td>Large leaf area, thin cuticle, many stomata → higher transpiration.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h3>2.5 Quantitative Expression</h3>
    <p>Transpiration rate (E) is often expressed as:</p>
    <p style="font-size:1.1em;">
        <strong>E = g_s × VPD</strong>
    </p>
    <p>where <strong>g_s</strong> = stomatal conductance (mol m⁻² s⁻¹) and <strong>VPD</strong> = vapor pressure deficit (kPa). Remember: <span style='color: var(--warning);'>VPD = saturation vapor pressure – actual vapor pressure</span>.</p>

    <h3>2.6 Mnemonic for Factors (T‑R‑W‑L‑S‑A)</h3>
    <p><strong>T</strong>emperature, <strong>R</strong>elative humidity, <strong>W</strong>ind, <strong>L</strong>ight, <strong>S</strong>oil water, <strong>A</strong>natomy – the six major determinants of transpiration.</p>

    <h3>2.7 Comparison: Respiration vs Transpiration</h3>
    <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; margin-bottom:15px;">
        <tr style="background:#dff0d8;">
            <th>Aspect</th>
            <th>Respiration</th>
            <th>Transpiration</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Primary Purpose</td>
            <td>Energy production (ATP)</td>
            <td>Water movement & cooling</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Location</td>
            <td>Mitochondria (cells)</td>
            <td>Leaves, stems (stomata, cuticle)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Products</td>
            <td>CO₂, H₂O, ATP</td>
            <td>Water vapor, transpiration pull</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Regulation</td>
            <td>Enzyme activity, substrate availability</td>
            <td>Guard‑cell turgor, environmental cues</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Effect on Plant</td>
            <td>Provides energy for growth, maintenance</td>
            <td>Enables mineral uptake, temperature regulation</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h3>2.8 Common Exam Questions & Quick Answers</h3>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Q:</strong> Write the overall equation for aerobic respiration in plants.<br>
            <strong>A:</strong> <code>C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6 O₂ → 6 CO₂ + 6 H₂O + 38 ATP</code></li>
        <li><strong>Q:</strong> List two mechanisms of water loss other than stomata.<br>
            <strong>A:</strong> Cuticular transpiration & lenticular (hydathode) transpiration.</li>
        <li><strong>Q:</strong> Explain why transpiration is higher in the afternoon.<br>
            <strong>A:</strong> Light intensity ↑ → stomata open; temperature ↑ → higher VPD; wind often stronger.</li>
        <li><strong>Q:</strong> Differentiate between root pressure and transpiration pull.<br>
            <strong>A:</strong> Root pressure is a positive pressure generated by active ion uptake in roots (works mainly at night); transpiration pull is a negative pressure (tension) created by water evaporation from leaves, driving ascent of sap (daytime).</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>2.9 Exam Tips for Transpiration</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Memorise the **percentage contribution** of each pathway – stomatal > cuticular.</li>
        <li>Always link guard‑cell physiology with environmental factors (light, ABA, humidity).</li>
        <li>When a question asks for “factors affecting rate”, present them in a **T‑R‑W‑L‑S‑A** order for quick recall.</li>
        <li>For diagram‑based questions, draw a leaf cross‑section showing stomata, cuticle, hydathodes, and label the water‑movement steps.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>3. Quick Revision Summary</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Respiration</strong> – 4 stages (Glycolysis, Link, Krebs, ETC); occurs in mitochondria; yields ~38 ATP; affected by temperature, O₂, substrate.</li>
        <li><strong>Transpiration</strong> – mainly stomatal (95‑99 %); driven by VPD; regulated by guard cells; essential for water uptake and cooling.</li>
        <li>Mnemonic <strong>GKE</strong> for respiration stages; <strong>T‑R‑W‑L‑S‑A</strong> for transpiration factors.</li>
        <li>Key equations: <code>C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6 O₂ → 6 CO₂ + 6 H₂O + 38 ATP</code> and <strong>E = g_s × VPD</strong>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["ai-gen-biology-ecosystem-components-food-chains-food-webs-ecological-pyramids"] = `
<div class="revision-card">

<h2>Ecosystem – Components, Food Chains, Food Webs & Ecological Pyramids</h2>

<h3><strong>1. What is an Ecosystem?</strong></h3>
<p>An <strong>ecosystem</strong> is a functional unit of ecology where the <strong>biotic (living)</strong> and <strong>abiotic (non-living)</strong> components interact as a system, forming a community of organisms and their environment. The term was coined by <strong>Arthur Tansley</strong> in <span style='color: var(--warning);'>1935</span>.</p>

<h3><strong>2. Components of an Ecosystem</strong></h3>

<h4><strong>A. Biotic Components (Living)</strong></h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Producers:</strong> Autotrophs (mainly green plants &amp; algae) that synthesize organic compounds via <strong>photosynthesis</strong> or <strong>chemosynthesis</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>Consumers:</strong> Heterotrophs that depend on producers for food. Classified into:</li>
<ul>
<li><strong>Herbivores (Primary consumers):</strong> e.g., deer, rabbits, insects.</li>
<li><strong>Carnivores (Secondary/tertiary consumers):</strong> e.g., lions, snakes.</li>
<li><strong>Omnivores:</strong> eat both plants and animals.</li>
<li><strong>Detritus feeders/scavengers:</strong> e.g., earthworms, vultures, fungi.</li>
</ul>
<li><strong>Decomposers:</strong> Break down dead organic matter into simpler substances, recycling nutrients. Examples: <strong>bacteria</strong>, <strong>fungi</strong>.</li>
</ul>

<h4><strong>B. Abiotic Components (Non-living)</strong></h4>
<ul>
<li>Physical factors: <strong>light</strong>, <strong>temperature</strong>, <strong>water</strong>, <strong>humidity</strong>, <strong>wind</strong>.</li>
<li>Chemical factors: <strong>minerals</strong>, <strong>gases</strong> (CO₂, O₂, N₂), <strong>pH</strong> of soil/water.</li>
</ul>

<h3><strong>3. Types of Ecosystems</strong></h3>
<table border="1" cellpadding="6" cellspacing="0">
<tr><th>Natural Ecosystem</th><th>Artificial/Man-made Ecosystem</th></tr>
<tr><td>Unchanged by humans</td><td>Created and maintained by humans</td></tr>
<tr><td>e.g., Forest, Lake, Desert</td><td>e.g., Agricultural field, Aquarium, Garden</td></tr>
</table>

<h3><strong>4. Food Chain</strong></h3>
<p>A <strong>food chain</strong> represents a linear sequence showing who eats whom in an ecosystem. There are generally <strong>5–7 trophic levels</strong>.</p>

<h4><strong>Types of Food Chains:</strong></h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Grassland Food Chain:</strong> Grass → Insect → Frog → Snake → Hawk.</li>
<li><strong>Marine Food Chain:</strong> Phytoplankton → Zooplankton → Small fish → Large fish → Man.</li>
<li><strong>Decomposer Chain:</strong> Dead plants → Bacteria/Fungi → Earthworm → Frog → Snake → Hawk.</li>
</ul>

<h3><strong>5. Food Web</strong></h3>
<p>A <strong>food web</strong> is a complex network formed by interconnecting multiple food chains, showing interdependence among species.</p>
<p><strong>Significance:</strong> Greater stability, biodiversity, resilience against environmental changes.</p>

<h3><strong>6. Trophic Levels</strong></h3>
<p>Feeding level in an ecosystem:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Trophic Level 1:</strong> Producers (plants, algae)</li>
<li><strong>Trophic Level 2:</strong> Primary consumers (herbivores)</li>
<li><strong>Trophic Level 3:</strong> Secondary consumers (carnivores)</li>
<li><strong>Trophic Level 4:</strong> Tertiary consumers (top carnivores)</li>
<li><strong>Trophic Level 5:</strong> Apex predators</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>10% Rule (Lindeman’s Efficiency):</strong> Only about <strong>10%</strong> energy is transferred from one trophic level to the next. Rest is lost as heat, waste, and undigested material.</p>

<h3><strong>7. Ecological Pyramids</strong></h3>
<p>Graphical representation of biomass, number or energy at successive trophic levels.</p>

<h4><strong>A. Pyramid of Numbers</strong></h4>
<p>Shows relative number of organisms at each trophic level.</p>
<ul>
<li>Can be <strong>upright</strong> (e.g., forest), <strong>inverted</strong> (e.g., tree parasites), or <strong>spindly</strong> (irregular) in shape.</li>
</ul>

<h4><strong>B. Pyramid of Biomass</strong></h4>
<p>Represents total dry weight (biomass) of organisms per unit area at each level.</p>
<ul>
<li>Usually <strong>upright</strong>, but may be <strong>inverted</strong> in aquatic ecosystems (e.g., phytoplankton have low biomass but high turnover).</li>
</ul>

<h4><strong>C. Pyramid of Energy</strong></h4>
<p>Always <strong>upright</strong>, as energy flow is unidirectional and decreases with each level.</p>

<h3><strong>Mnemonic for Food Chain Order:</strong></h3>
<p><em>“Plants Convert Herbivores Into Carnivores” → P-C-H-I-C</em></p>

<h3><strong>Key Comparisons – Ecosystem Terms</strong></h3>
<table border="1" cellpadding="6" cellspacing="0">
<tr><th>Food Chain</th><th>Food Web</th></tr>
<tr><td>Linear sequence</td><td>Branching, complex network</td></tr>
<tr><td>Shows single pathway</td><td>Shows interconnections</td></tr>
<tr><td>Less stable</td><td>More stable</td></tr>
</table>

<h3><strong>Exam Tips:</strong></h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Lindeman’s Efficiency:</strong> Always remember the <strong>10% rule</strong> applies across energy pyramids.</li>
<li><strong>Pyramid of Energy is always upright</strong> – important for exam questions.</li>
<li><strong>Tansley</strong> introduced the concept of ecosystem (<span style='color: var(--warning);'>1935</span>).</li>
<li><strong>Decomposers</strong> are <strong>not always part of visible food chains</strong> but crucial for nutrient cycling.</li>
<li><strong>Marine ecosystems</strong> often start with <strong>phytoplankton</strong>; terrestrial with <strong>grasses/trees</strong>.</li>
<li>Distinguish between <strong>detritus feeder</strong> and <strong>scavenger</strong>: former consumes detritus, latter eats carrion.</li>
</ul>

<h3><strong>Summary Cheat Sheet</strong></h3>
<table border="1" cellpadding="6" cellspacing="0">
<tr><th>Term</th><th>Definition / Key Point</th></tr>
<tr><td>Ecosystem</td><td>Biotic + Abiotic interaction unit</td></tr>
<tr><td>Producers</td><td>Autotrophs (plants, algae)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Consumers</td><td>Herbivores, carnivores, omnivores</td></tr>
<tr><td>Decomposers</td><td>Bacteria, fungi – recycle matter</td></tr>
<tr><td>Food Chain</td><td>Linear – who eats whom</td></tr>
<tr><td>Food Web</td><td>Interconnected food chains</td></tr>
<tr><td>Trophic Level</td><td>Position in food chain</td></tr>
<tr><td>Energy Transfer</td><td><strong>~10%</strong> efficiency (Lindeman)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pyramid of Energy</td><td>Always upright</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pyramid of Biomass</td><td>May be inverted (aquatic)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pyramid of Numbers</td><td>May be upright, inverted, or spindly</td></tr>
</table>

<p><strong>Note:</strong> In competitive exams, expect questions on pyramid shapes in different ecosystems and significance of the 10% energy loss law.</p>

</div>
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["ai-gen-biology-environment-pollution-types-effects-remediation"] = `
<div class="revision-card">
    <h2>Environment &amp; Pollution – Types, Effects &amp; Remediation</h2>

    <h3>1. Why the Topic is Important for NDA/CDS/AFCAT</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Understanding the impact of pollution on human health and national security (e.g., water scarcity, disease outbreaks).</li>
        <li>Questions often ask for <strong>classification</strong>, <strong>major sources</strong>, and <strong>control measures</strong>.</li>
        <li>Exam tip: Memorise the **5‑R** mnemonic for remediation – <strong>Reduce, Reuse, Recycle, Recover, Remediate</strong>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2. Classification of Pollution</h3>
    <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%;">
        <thead style="background:#f2f2f2;">
            <tr>
                <th>Type</th>
                <th>Medium Affected</th>
                <th>Major Sources</th>
                <th>Key Indicators</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Air Pollution</strong></td>
                <td>Atmosphere</td>
                <td>Industrial emissions, vehicular exhaust, biomass burning, power plants</td>
                <td>PM<sub>2.5</sub>, PM<sub>10</sub>, SO₂, NOₓ, CO, O₃</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Water Pollution</strong></td>
                <td>Freshwater &amp; Marine</td>
                <td>Domestic sewage, agro‑chemicals, oil spills, heavy metals</td>
                <td>BOD, COD, DO, pH, nitrate, phosphate, coliform count</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Soil Pollution</strong></td>
                <td>Terrestrial</td>
                <td>Pesticides, industrial waste dumps, mining tailings, landfill leachate</td>
                <td>Heavy metal concentration, organic contaminant levels, soil pH</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Noise Pollution</strong></td>
                <td>Acoustic environment</td>
                <td>Traffic, aircraft, industrial machinery, urban construction</td>
                <td>Decibel (dB) levels – <span style='color: var(--warning);'>>85 dB</span> harmful over 8 hrs</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Thermal Pollution</strong></td>
                <td>Water bodies &amp; atmosphere</td>
                <td>Cooling water discharge from power plants, urban heat islands</td>
                <td>ΔT (temperature rise) – <span style='color: var(--warning);'>>2 °C</span> detrimental to aquatic life</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Radioactive Pollution</strong></td>
                <td>Soil, water, air</td>
                <td>Nuclear accidents, medical waste, uranium mining</td>
                <td>Radiation dose – <span style='color: var(--warning);'>>1 mSv/yr</span> above background</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>3. Major Global Milestones (Dates – highlighted)</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><span style='color: var(--warning);'>1972</span> – Stockholm Conference on the Human Environment (first global environmental summit).</li>
        <li><span style='color: var(--warning);'>1992</span> – Rio Earth Summit – Agenda 21 and the concept of Sustainable Development.</li>
        <li><span style='color: var(--warning);'>1997</span> – Kyoto Protocol – legally binding targets for greenhouse gas reduction.</li>
        <li><span style='color: var(--warning);'>2015</span> – Paris Agreement – aim to limit global warming to <strong>2 °C</strong> above pre‑industrial levels.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Effects of Pollution – A Quick Reference</h3>
    <h4>4.1 Air Pollution</h4>
    <ul>
        <li>Respiratory diseases – asthma, chronic bronchitis, lung cancer.</li>
        <li>Acid rain – leaches calcium from soils, damages forests &amp; monuments.</li>
        <li>Global warming – greenhouse gases (CO₂, CH₄, N₂O) trap heat.</li>
        <li>Visibility reduction – smog formation (photochemical smog).</li>
    </ul>

    <h4>4.2 Water Pollution</h4>
    <ul>
        <li>Eutrophication – algal blooms, hypoxia, fish kills.</li>
        <li>Bioaccumulation of heavy metals (Hg, Pb, Cd) in the food chain.</li>
        <li>Water‑borne diseases – cholera, dysentery, hepatitis A.</li>
        <li>Corrosion of infrastructure due to acidic water.</li>
    </ul>

    <h4>4.3 Soil Pollution</h4>
    <ul>
        <li>Loss of fertility – pesticide residues hinder microbial activity.</li>
        <li>Groundwater contamination – leaching of nitrates &amp; heavy metals.</li>
        <li>Reduced crop yield &amp; quality – economic impact on agriculture.</li>
    </ul>

    <h4>4.4 Noise Pollution</h4>
    <ul>
        <li>Hearing loss, hypertension, sleep disturbance.</li>
        <li>Impact on wildlife – disruption of communication &amp; breeding patterns.</li>
    </ul>

    <h4>4.5 Thermal Pollution</h4>
    <ul>
        <li>Decreased dissolved oxygen in water → fish mortality.</li>
        <li>Altered species composition – favoring thermophilic organisms.</li>
    </ul>

    <h4>4.6 Radioactive Pollution</h4>
    <ul>
        <li>Genetic mutations, cancers, birth defects.</li>
        <li>Long‑term land uninhabitability – e.g., Chernobyl Exclusion Zone.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>5. Remediation &amp; Control Techniques</h3>
    <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; margin-top:10px;">
        <thead style="background:#e6f7ff;">
            <tr>
                <th>Pollution Type</th>
                <th>Primary Remediation Method</th>
                <th>Key Advantages</th>
                <th>Exam‑Friendly Mnemonic</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Air</strong></td>
                <td>Scrubbers (wet/dry), Electrostatic precipitators, Catalytic converters, Afforestation</td>
                <td>Immediate reduction of particulates &amp; gases; can be retro‑fitted.</td>
                <td><strong>SCAR</strong> – <em>Scrubbers, Catalytic, Air‑filters, Re‑plant</em></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Water</strong></td>
                <td>Primary treatment (screening, sedimentation), Secondary (activated sludge, bio‑filters), Tertiary (UV, ozonation, membrane filtration), Phytoremediation</td>
                <td>Progressive removal of BOD, pathogens, and chemicals.</td>
                <td><strong>PSTP</strong> – <em>Primary, Secondary, Tertiary, Phytoremediation</em></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Soil</strong></td>
                <td>Bioremediation (microbes, fungi), Phytoremediation (hyperaccumulators), Soil washing, Land‑filling, Encapsulation</td>
                <td>Cost‑effective, environmentally friendly, restores fertility.</td>
                <td><strong>BPSL</strong> – <em>Bioremediation, Phyto, Soil‑wash, Land‑fill</em></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Noise</strong></td>
                <td>Barriers (walls, earth mounds), Low‑noise road surfaces, Zoning regulations, Personal protective equipment (PPE)</td>
                <td>Simple engineering controls; immediate effect.</td>
                <td><strong>BLZP</strong> – <em>Barriers, Low‑noise, Zoning, PPE</em></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Thermal</strong></td>
                <td>Cooling towers, Closed‑loop cooling, Wet‑land creation, Heat exchangers</td>
                <td>Reduces temperature rise in receiving waters.</td>
                <td><strong>CWH</strong> – <em>Cooling, Wet‑land, Heat‑exchanger</em></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Radioactive</strong></td>
                <td>Containment (concrete vaults), Soil immobilization (cementation), Phytoremediation (radionuclide‑accumulating plants), Deep geological disposal</td>
                <td>Long‑term isolation of radionuclides.</td>
                <td><strong>ICPD</strong> – <em>Immobilize, Contain, Phyto, Deep‑store</em></td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>6. Comparative Snapshot – Developed vs. Developing Nations</h3>
    <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; margin-top:10px;">
        <thead style="background:#fff3e0;">
            <tr>
                <th>Aspect</th>
                <th>Developed Countries</th>
                <th>Developing Countries</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Primary Pollutants</strong></td>
                <td>Industrial emissions, vehicular NOₓ &amp; CO₂</td>
                <td>Agricultural runoff, open burning, unregulated waste dumping</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Regulatory Framework</strong></td>
                <td>Stringent standards (e.g., EPA, EU Directives)</td>
                <td>Emerging policies; enforcement gaps</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Technology Adoption</strong></td>
                <td>Advanced scrubbers, renewable energy integration</td>
                <td>Gradual shift; reliance on low‑cost solutions</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Health Impact</strong></td>
                <td>Chronic diseases, aging population vulnerability</td>
                <td>Acute outbreaks, child mortality, occupational exposure</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>7. Quick Mnemonics for Exams</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Air Pollution Sources</strong> – <em>VIBES</em>: <strong>V</strong>ehicles, <strong>I</strong>ndustries, <strong>B</strong>iomass, <strong>E</strong>nergy plants, <strong>S</strong>olvents.</li>
        <li><strong>Water Pollution Types</strong> – <em>CHOP</em>: <strong>C</strong>hemical, <strong>H</strong>eavy metals, <strong>O</strong>il, <strong>P</strong>athogenic.</li>
        <li><strong>Soil Remediation</strong> – <em>RICH</em>: <strong>R</strong>emediation, <strong>I</strong>mprovement, <strong>C</strong>ontainment, <strong>H</strong>arvest (phytoremediation).</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>8. Exam Tips &amp; Tricks</h3>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Never forget the 5‑R’s</strong> – they appear in multiple choice questions on mitigation.</li>
        <li>When a question asks for “major source”, think of the **VIBES** or **CHOP** mnemonics first.</li>
        <li>For “effects on ecosystem”, link the pollutant to its **key indicator** (e.g., high BOD → low DO → fish kill).</li>
        <li>Use the comparative table to quickly eliminate wrong options in “developed vs. developing” type questions.</li>
        <li>Remember the **global milestones** – dates are often asked as “When was the Kyoto Protocol adopted?” – answer: <span style='color: var(--warning);'>1997</span>.</li>
        <li>Write short, crisp answers: start with the **definition**, follow with **sources**, then **effects**, and end with **remediation** – this structure scores marks in descriptive questions.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>9. Summary – One‑Liner Cheat Sheet</h3>
    <p><strong>Pollution = Source → Medium → Indicator → Effect → Control.</strong> Keep the flow in mind and you’ll ace any question.</p>
</div>
`;

